@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@cindex introduction
@cindex overview

Welcome.  Vellumory lets you read PDF documents in Emacs as comfortably as
in a dedicated viewer@comma{} and then do more with them than most viewers
allow: inspect how a document is built@comma{} check it against archival and
accessibility standards@comma{} and change it without ever losing an earlier
version.

This chapter explains what Vellumory is and what it is not.  If you would
rather start reading right away@comma{} skip to @ref{Getting started}.

@menu
* What Vellumory is::
* Why it exists::
* What Vellumory is not::
* A first session::
* About this manual::
@end menu

@node What Vellumory is
@section What Vellumory is

@cindex worker program
@cindex architecture

Vellumory is a document reader for GNU Emacs.  It shows PDF pages in an
ordinary Emacs buffer@comma{} sharp and continuous@comma{} and keeps up while you
scroll.  When the pages are too wide for your window@comma{} or you would simply
rather read text@comma{} its reading mode reflows the document into one column
of text that fits the window.  It also reads XPS@comma{} EPUB@comma{} CBZ comic books and FB2 books@comma{} and@comma{}
through converters you may already have installed@comma{} DVI@comma{} PostScript@comma{} DjVu
and office documents.

It is made of three parts that work together:

@itemize
@item
An Emacs Lisp package.  This is the reader you see: the pages@comma{} the
sidebars@comma{} search@comma{} selection@comma{} the menus@comma{} Org links@comma{} evil keys and a Doom
Emacs module.
@item
A worker program called @code{vellumory}@comma{} written in Lean 4 with a small C
bridge to the MuPDF library.  Emacs starts it in the background.  It
renders pages on several threads@comma{} extracts text@comma{} reads the document's
structure and carries out edits.
@item
The same program as a command line tool.  Scripts can inspect@comma{} validate@comma{}
compare and edit documents exactly as the reader does.
@end itemize

The name joins @emph{vellum}@comma{} the prepared skin that medieval books were written
on@comma{} and @emph{memory}: Vellumory remembers where you were in every document@comma{}
and every state a document has ever been in.

@node Why it exists
@section Why it exists

@cindex motivation

Reading inside Emacs keeps documents next to your notes@comma{} your code and
your mail.  Until now that came with compromises.  DocView converts pages
with external programs before you see them.  pdf-tools renders a page at
a time on Emacs's main thread@comma{} so large or complex pages make Emacs wait.

Vellumory was built to remove those compromises:

@itemize
@item
Pages appear quickly and stay sharp at your screen's resolution.  Only
the parts of pages that a window shows are rendered at full resolution@comma{}
in parallel@comma{} away from Emacs's main thread.  While you scroll faster
than pages can be rendered@comma{} quick previews fill in and sharpen as soon
as you stop.
@item
Your work is safe.  Every change to a document is recorded.  You can
undo it@comma{} redo it@comma{} or go back to any earlier state@comma{} byte for byte.
@item
Reading adapts to you.  Page colours follow a dark theme@comma{} a reading
mode reflows the text to any window width@comma{} and resting the mouse on a
reference shows where it leads without leaving your place.
@item
Documents are transparent.  A report shows fonts@comma{} images@comma{} metadata@comma{}
attachments@comma{} signatures and claimed standards.  Validation with veraPDF
tells you whether a document really is PDF/A or PDF/UA@.
@item
Claims are measured.  The speed figures in this manual come with the
method used to obtain them@comma{} so you can repeat them on your own machine
(see @ref{Performance and how it is measured}).
@end itemize

@node What Vellumory is not
@section What Vellumory is not

@cindex limitations
@cindex what is missing

It is only fair to say plainly what Vellumory does not do@comma{} so that you are
not surprised later.

@itemize
@item
It is not a page-layout editor.  You cannot change the text printed on a
page@comma{} move images or draw.  Vellumory's edits work on the document's
structure: pages@comma{} metadata@comma{} attachments@comma{} annotations on selected text@comma{}
notes@comma{} form values@comma{} stamps@comma{} encryption@comma{} text recognition and@comma{} for
experts@comma{} the raw PDF syntax.
@item
It needs a graphical Emacs.  Pages are images@comma{} so a text terminal cannot
show them.
@item
It is not yet in a package archive such as MELPA@.  You build the worker
program yourself; Nix makes that one command (see @ref{Installation}).
@item
It never runs JavaScript embedded in documents.  It cannot show dynamic
XFA forms@comma{} because MuPDF does not render them.
@item
It verifies digital signatures but cannot sign documents.
@item
It has no print command.  Print the file with your system's tools.
@item
It needs Emacs 29.1 or newer.  Exporting Org to accessible PDF needs
Org 9.7 or newer (Emacs 30's@comma{} or Org from GNU ELPA).
@item
It is young.  This manual describes version 0.1.0@comma{} its first release.
It has been developed and tested on Linux (x86@math{_64})@comma{} with Emacs 31.1 and
the development version of Emacs 32.
@end itemize

Some properties of the worker are proved in Lean: 23 theorems about the
render scheduler@comma{} the edit history@comma{} page geometry@comma{} argument passing@comma{}
navigation and the comparison algorithm.  They are checked to use only
Lean's standard axioms.  They do not prove MuPDF@comma{} qpdf or veraPDF correct@comma{}
and they are no substitute for the tests.

@node A first session
@section A first session

@cindex tour
@cindex first session

Here is what a first session can look like.  The keys are explained in
the chapters that follow.

@enumerate
@item
Visit a PDF with @code{C-x C-f}.  The first page appears@comma{} with page previews
in a sidebar on the left.
@item
Press @code{SPC} to read on and @code{DEL} to go back.  @code{n} and @code{p} turn whole
pages.
@item
Type @code{/ trace RET}.  Every match of "trace" is highlighted on the
pages; @code{M-n} moves to the next one.
@item
Drag over a sentence with the mouse and press @code{M-w}.  The text is in
the kill ring@comma{} ready to paste into your notes.
@item
With the sentence still selected@comma{} press @code{a h}.  The sentence is now
highlighted in the file itself@comma{} as an annotation.
@item
Changed your mind?  @code{C-/} undoes the edit and puts the file back
exactly as it was.
@item
Press @code{C-c C-r} to read the text reflowed to your window@comma{} in a size and
colours that suit you; @code{q} takes you back to the pages@comma{} at the place you
reached.
@item
Press @code{?} to see every command in one menu.
@item
Press @code{q} to put the document away.  The next time you open it@comma{} it
opens where you left off.
@end enumerate

@node About this manual
@section About this manual

@cindex conventions

Keys are written as Emacs writes them: @code{C-x} means hold Control and press
x@comma{} @code{M-x} means Meta (usually Alt) and x.  @code{RET} is the Return key@comma{} @code{SPC} the
space bar and @code{DEL} the Backspace key.  Every command can also be run by
name with @code{M-x}.

The keys in the main chapters are those of plain Emacs.  If you use evil
or Doom Emacs@comma{} see @ref{Evil and Doom Emacs} for their keys; the commands are
the same.

Where a feature has limits@comma{} the section says so.  If you find that the
manual and the program disagree@comma{} the program is right and the manual has
a bug: please report it.

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

@cindex installation

Vellumory has two parts to install: the Emacs Lisp package and the worker
program it talks to.  The Nix flake builds both in one step@comma{} and it is the
tested way.  The other recipes in this chapter install the Lisp from the
source repository; you then build the worker separately.

The source repository is @uref{https://github.com/cypl0x/vellumory}.

