@node Requirements
@section Requirements

@cindex requirements
@cindex Emacs version
@cindex graphical display

You need:

@itemize
@item
GNU Emacs 29.1 or newer@comma{} running on a graphical display (X@comma{} Wayland or
the pgtk build) with image support.  In a text terminal the reader
refuses to start with the message "Vellumory needs a graphical Emacs
with image support".  Vellumory was developed on Emacs 31.1 and the
development version@comma{} Emacs 32@comma{} where its graphical tests run too.  Its
Emacs test suite also passes in batch mode on Emacs 29.4 and 30.2@comma{} and
the project's continuous integration is set up to run it on 29.4@comma{} 30.2@comma{}
31.1 and the development snapshot.
@item
For exporting Org to accessible@comma{} archival PDF: Org 9.7 or newer@comma{} which
is Emacs 30's; on Emacs 29 install Org from GNU ELPA@.
@item
Linux.  The Nix flake builds for @samp{x86_64-linux} and @samp{aarch64-linux}.  All
tests and measurements in this manual were made on x86@math{_64} Linux.  Other
systems have not been tried.
@item
The worker program @code{vellumory} (see @ref{The worker program}).
@end itemize

Some features use other programs.  Each one is optional; without it@comma{} only
the features in its row are unavailable.

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Program
@tab What it enables
@item qpdf (with its fix-qdf)
@tab every edit@comma{} extracting@comma{} splitting@comma{} merging@comma{} syntax view
@item veraPDF
@tab validation@comma{} batch validation@comma{} checks after Org export
@item ocrmypdf with Tesseract
@tab text recognition (OCR)
@item Lua@LaTeX{} from @TeX{} Live 2025 or newer
@tab Org export to accessible PDF through @LaTeX{}
@item Typst 0.14 or newer and the ox-typst package
@tab Org export through Typst
@item dvipdfmx
@tab reading DVI files
@item ps2pdf (Ghostscript)
@tab reading PostScript
@item ddjvu (DjVuLibre)
@tab reading DjVu
@item soffice (LibreOffice)
@tab reading office documents
@end multitable

In the Nix build@comma{} the worker has the exact paths of qpdf@comma{} fix-qdf@comma{} veraPDF
and ocrmypdf compiled in@comma{} and ocrmypdf brings its own Tesseract.  Built any
other way@comma{} it finds them on your @samp{PATH}.  @code{M-x vellumory-doctor} tells you
which ones it finds (see @ref{Checking that it works}).

Several Emacs packages add to Vellumory when you have them@comma{} and none is
required: evil@comma{} Doom Emacs@comma{} AUC@TeX{}@comma{} org-noter@comma{} consult@comma{} embark and
ox-typst.  The transient library@comma{} which draws the command menu@comma{} comes with
Emacs.

@node The worker program
@section The worker program

@cindex worker program
@vindex vellumory-program

The worker is the program that does the heavy lifting.  The reader starts
it with @code{vellumory serve} when you open your first document.  One worker
serves every document of your Emacs session@comma{} sharing its render threads by
priority@comma{} and it exits when you close the last document.

The option @code{vellumory-program} names it.  By default Vellumory looks for the
worker next to its own Lisp files@comma{} in two places:

@itemize
@item
In a Nix build@comma{} the Lisp lives in @samp{share/emacs/site-lisp/vellumory} and the
worker in @samp{bin/vellumory} of the same package.
@item
In a source checkout@comma{} the Lisp lives in @samp{lisp/} and the worker is built in
@samp{.lake/build/bin/vellumory}.
@end itemize

If neither exists@comma{} Vellumory runs whatever @code{vellumory} your @samp{exec-path}
finds.  If you keep the worker elsewhere@comma{} set the option:

@lisp
(setq vellumory-program "/path/to/bin/vellumory")
@end lisp

@menu
* Building the worker with Nix::
* Building the worker in the development shell::
* Building without Nix::
@end menu

@node Building the worker with Nix
@subsection Building the worker with Nix

@cindex Nix, building the worker

In a checkout of the repository:

@example
git clone https://github.com/cypl0x/vellumory
cd vellumory
nix build path:.
@end example

This builds the worker and the Lisp@comma{} and runs the native test suite and
the Emacs tests as part of the build@comma{} so the first build takes a while.
The result is a link @samp{result} with the worker @samp{result/bin/vellumory}@comma{} and in
@samp{result/share/emacs/site-lisp/vellumory} the Lisp@comma{} its generated autoloads
and this manual.

@node Building the worker in the development shell
@subsection Building the worker in the development shell

@cindex development shell

For working on Vellumory itself@comma{} the flake has a development shell with
every tool the tests need:

@example
nix develop path:.
lean --run scripts/Build.lean prepare
lean --run scripts/Build.lean lake build vellumory
@end example

The worker is then @samp{.lake/build/bin/vellumory}@comma{} which the Lisp in @samp{lisp/}
of the same checkout finds by itself.  A checkout has no autoloads file;
make one with @samp{lean --run scripts/Build.lean autoloads}@comma{} which writes
@samp{lisp/vellumory-autoloads.el}.

@node Building without Nix
@subsection Building without Nix

@cindex building without Nix

Building without Nix is possible in principle but not documented or
tested yet.  You would need Lean 4.30.0 with Lake@comma{} the lean4-cli library
(@samp{lean --run scripts/Build.lean prepare PATH} copies it into the checkout)@comma{}
MuPDF 1.27.2 (the worker links @samp{libmupdf.so.27.2})@comma{} OpenSSL@comma{} @TeX{} Live's
libsynctex and Exempi.  @samp{lakefile.lean} takes their locations as @samp{-K}
options (@samp{mupdfInclude}@comma{} @samp{mupdfLib}@comma{} @samp{opensslInclude} and so on)@comma{} and
@samp{scripts/Build.lean} shows how the pieces fit together.  If you try it@comma{}
your notes would be very welcome.

Prebuilt worker programs are planned but not available yet.

@node Nix flake
@section Nix flake

@cindex Nix flake

The flake offers these outputs@comma{} for @samp{x86_64-linux} and @samp{aarch64-linux}:

@table @asis
@item @code{packages.SYSTEM.default} (also called @code{vellumory})
the worker in
@samp{bin/}@comma{} and the Lisp@comma{} its generated autoloads and this manual in
@samp{share/emacs/site-lisp/vellumory}.
@item @code{packages.SYSTEM.lisp}
the Emacs Lisp package alone@comma{} byte-compiled by
Emacs's own package builder@comma{} without the worker.
@item @code{devShells.SYSTEM.default}
every tool the build@comma{} the tests and the
benchmarks use.
@item @code{checks.SYSTEM.tooling}
the full build with its tests.
@end table

The other packages (the test documents and the Doom review build) serve
the project's own tests and benchmarks.  There is no overlay and no NixOS
or home-manager module yet.

The simplest way to use a build is to add its Lisp directory to your
@samp{load-path}:

@lisp
(add-to-list 'load-path "/path/to/result/share/emacs/site-lisp/vellumory")
(require 'vellumory-autoloads)
(vellumory-install)
@end lisp

Because the worker sits beside that Lisp directory@comma{} Vellumory finds it
without further settings@comma{} and @code{vellumory-info} finds the manual there too.

In a home-manager configuration@comma{} the same three lines with the package's
store path do the job.  This sketch has not been tested:

@example
# flake.nix: inputs.vellumory.url = "github:cypl0x/vellumory";
@{ inputs@comma{} pkgs@comma{} ... @}:
let
  vellumory = inputs.vellumory.packages.$@{pkgs.stdenv.hostPlatform.system@}.default;
in @{
  home.packages = [ vellumory ];   # the vellumory command line tool
  programs.emacs = @{
    enable = true;
    extraConfig = ''
      (add-to-list 'load-path "$@{vellumory@}/share/emacs/site-lisp/vellumory")
      (require 'vellumory-autoloads)
      (vellumory-install)
    '';
  @};
@}
@end example

If you use the @code{lisp} package instead@comma{} as an ordinary Emacs package@comma{} it has
no worker beside it: put the worker on your @samp{PATH} or set
@code{vellumory-program}.

@cindex OCR languages
@cindex Tesseract languages
Text recognition comes with every language Tesseract knows@comma{} which takes
about a gigabyte.  To keep only the languages you read@comma{} override the
package:

@example
vellumory.override @{ tesseractLanguages = [ "eng" "deu" ]; @}
@end example

Tesseract's orientation and script detection (@samp{osd})@comma{} which ocrmypdf uses@comma{}
is always kept.

@node Doom Emacs
@section Doom Emacs

@cindex Doom Emacs, installing

The repository contains a Doom module@comma{} @samp{:tools vellumory}@comma{} in
@samp{doom/tools/vellumory}.  It replaces Doom's @samp{:tools pdf}; enable one of the
two@comma{} not both.

@enumerate
@item
Clone the repository and build the worker (see @ref{The worker program})@comma{} for example in @samp{~/src/vellumory}.
@item
Link the module into your private modules:
@example
mkdir -p "$@{DOOMDIR:-$HOME/.config/doom@}/modules/tools"
ln -s ~/src/vellumory/doom/tools/vellumory \
      "$@{DOOMDIR:-$HOME/.config/doom@}/modules/tools/vellumory"
@end example
@item
In @samp{init.el}@comma{} add @samp{vellumory} under @samp{:tools} (and remove @samp{pdf}).  The
module has no flags.
@item
Tell Vellumory where the worker is@comma{} in @samp{config.el}:
@lisp
(setq vellumory-program (expand-file-name "~/src/vellumory/result/bin/vellumory"))
@end lisp
@item
Run @samp{doom sync}@comma{} restart Emacs@comma{} and run @samp{doom doctor}.
@end enumerate

The module's @samp{packages.el} installs the Lisp with Doom's usual package
manager (straight.el) from GitHub; if the environment variable
@samp{VELLUMORY_SOURCE} names a checkout@comma{} it builds from that checkout instead.
With nix-doom-emacs-unstraightened@comma{} the module leaves the package to Nix:
add the flake's @code{lisp} output to @samp{extraPackages} and its @code{default} output to
@samp{extraBinPackages}@comma{} which puts the worker on Emacs's @samp{PATH}.

@samp{doom doctor} runs the same checks as @code{M-x vellumory-doctor} (see @ref{Checking that it works})@comma{} reporting errors as errors and warnings as warnings.  It
also warns if @samp{:tools pdf} is enabled too@comma{} and if @TeX{} Live lacks the @LaTeX{}
tagging code that Org export needs.

What the module sets up is described in @ref{The Doom module}.  The route
through nix-doom-emacs-unstraightened is the one the project tests; the
straight.el route from GitHub has not been tested yet.

@node straightel
@section straight.el

@cindex straight.el

@lisp
(straight-use-package
 '(vellumory :type git :host github :repo "cypl0x/vellumory"
             :files ("lisp/*.el")))
(setq vellumory-program (expand-file-name "~/src/vellumory/result/bin/vellumory"))
(vellumory-install)
@end lisp

straight.el generates and loads the autoloads itself.  Build the worker
separately (see @ref{The worker program}); the path above assumes a Nix build
in @samp{~/src/vellumory}.  This recipe follows straight.el's documented format
but has not been tested.

@node Elpaca
@section Elpaca

@cindex Elpaca

@lisp
(use-package vellumory
  :ensure (:host github :repo "cypl0x/vellumory" :files ("lisp/*.el"))
  :defer t
  :init
  (setq vellumory-program (expand-file-name "~/src/vellumory/result/bin/vellumory"))
  (vellumory-install))
@end lisp

As with straight.el@comma{} build the worker separately.  This recipe has not
been tested.

@node package-vc and use-package
@section package-vc and use-package

@cindex package-vc
@cindex use-package

Emacs can install packages straight from their repository with
package-vc@comma{} and since Emacs 30 use-package can too.  Vellumory's Lisp lives
in the @samp{lisp} subdirectory@comma{} and its manual in @samp{docs}:

@lisp
(package-vc-install
 '(vellumory :url "https://github.com/cypl0x/vellumory"
             :lisp-dir "lisp"
             :doc "docs/vellumory.texi"))
(vellumory-install)
@end lisp

With use-package's @samp{:vc} keyword:

@lisp
(use-package vellumory
  :vc (:url "https://github.com/cypl0x/vellumory"
       :lisp-dir "lisp" :doc "docs/vellumory.texi" :rev :newest)
  :defer t
  :init (vellumory-install))
@end lisp

The @samp{:doc} entry builds this manual and installs it into Info@comma{} so
@code{vellumory-info} finds it@comma{} and package-vc generates the autoloads.
package-vc keeps the whole repository@comma{} so if you build the worker inside
that checkout (in the development shell@comma{} see @ref{Building the worker in the development shell})@comma{} Vellumory finds it there without further settings;
otherwise set @code{vellumory-program}.  These recipes follow the documented
package-vc format but have not been tested.

@node Spacemacs
@section Spacemacs

@cindex Spacemacs

There is no Spacemacs layer yet; one is planned.  Until then@comma{} Spacemacs
can install the package from its repository.  In @samp{dotspacemacs/layers}:

@lisp
dotspacemacs-additional-packages
'((vellumory :location (recipe :fetcher github
                               :repo "cypl0x/vellumory"
                               :files ("lisp/*.el"))))
@end lisp

Then@comma{} in @samp{dotspacemacs/user-config}@comma{} point Vellumory at the worker you
built and install it:

@lisp
(setq vellumory-program (expand-file-name "~/src/vellumory/result/bin/vellumory"))
(vellumory-install)
(with-eval-after-load 'evil (require 'vellumory-evil) (vellumory-evil-setup))
@end lisp

This has not been tested.

@node Guix
@section Guix

@cindex Guix

A Guix package does not exist yet.  It would need to build the worker
with Lean 4.30 and MuPDF 1.27.2.

@node Package archives
@section Package archives

@cindex MELPA
@cindex GNU ELPA

Vellumory is not yet in MELPA@comma{} GNU ELPA or NonGNU ELPA@.  Submitting it is
planned.  Even from an archive@comma{} the worker will still have to be
installed separately@comma{} because it is a compiled program; prebuilt worker
programs are planned too.

@node Setting up your init file
@section Setting up your init file

@cindex init file
@cindex file associations

Once the package is on your @samp{load-path}@comma{} two lines are enough:

@lisp
(require 'vellumory-autoloads)   ; skip this if your package manager loads it
(vellumory-install)
@end lisp

The autoloads file is generated@comma{} not part of the repository.  Package
managers make their own@comma{} and the Nix package contains one.  In a plain
clone@comma{} make it once in the clone's top directory@comma{} with
@samp{lean --run scripts/Build.lean autoloads} in the development shell@comma{} or
with Emacs alone:

@example
emacs --batch -Q --eval '(loaddefs-generate "lisp" (expand-file-name "lisp/vellumory-autoloads.el") (list "vellumory-autoloads.el") nil nil t)'
@end example

@table @asis
@item @kbd{M-x vellumory-install}
@findex vellumory-install
Make Emacs open documents in Vellumory.  It
  associates the extensions in @code{vellumory-file-extensions} and every file
  that starts with @samp{%PDF} with @code{vellumory-mode}.  It also associates the
  converted formats whose converter is installed (except those in
  @code{vellumory-converted-not-visited})@comma{} installs a file handler that visits
  documents without reading their bytes into the buffer@comma{} skips Emacs's
  large-file question for documents@comma{} prepares Org links@comma{} the AUC@TeX{} viewer
  and org-noter for when those load@comma{} and binds @code{;} in Dired (see
  @code{vellumory-dired-key}).  It does not load the reader itself; that
  happens when you open the first document.
@item @kbd{M-x vellumory-uninstall}
@findex vellumory-uninstall
Undo all of that.
@end table

You do not have to install anything to try Vellumory: @code{M-x vellumory-open}
reads any file in Vellumory@comma{} associations or not.

If you also use pdf-tools or DocView@comma{} the reader installed last wins@comma{}
because each adds its association to the front of Emacs's lists.  Call
@code{vellumory-install} after @code{pdf-tools-install} if you want Vellumory to open
PDFs.

For evil@comma{} add:

@lisp
(with-eval-after-load 'evil
  (require 'vellumory-evil)
  (vellumory-evil-setup))
@end lisp

@node Reading this manual in Emacs
@section Reading this manual in Emacs

@cindex Info manual

@table @asis
@item @kbd{M-x vellumory-info}
@findex vellumory-info
Open this manual in Info.  It is also on @code{M} in
  the command menu (@code{?}) of a document@comma{} and on @code{m} in that menu elsewhere.
@end table

@code{vellumory-info} looks for @samp{vellumory.info} beside Vellumory's Lisp files
first@comma{} where the Nix package installs it@comma{} and then in the directories
Info searches@comma{} where the package-vc recipes above install it.  The @code{lisp}
package of the flake and the Doom module's straight.el recipe do not
install it.

To build it from a checkout@comma{} run this in the development shell (it needs
@samp{makeinfo} from GNU Texinfo):

@example
lean --run scripts/Build.lean manual
@end example

That writes @samp{target/vellumory.info}.  Then tell Info where it is:

@lisp
(add-to-list 'Info-additional-directory-list "/path/to/vellumory/target/")
@end lisp

You can also build the manual anywhere you like with
@samp{makeinfo --no-split -o vellumory.info docs/vellumory.texi}.

@node Checking that it works
@section Checking that it works

@cindex checking the installation
@cindex doctor
@findex vellumory-doctor-mode

Vellumory can check its own setup and tell you what to fix.

@table @asis
@item @kbd{M-x vellumory-doctor}
@findex vellumory-doctor
Check everything Vellumory relies on and
  show the results in a report.  It is also @code{!} in the command menu.
@end table

The report has six sections:

@table @asis
@item Emacs
the version@comma{} a graphical frame@comma{} image support@comma{} PNG support@comma{} the
display@comma{} and how garbage collection is handled;
@item Worker
whether the worker program is found@comma{} answers@comma{} speaks the
protocol this version of the Lisp expects@comma{} and renders with MuPDF;
@item Opening PDF files
whether PDF files really open in Vellumory@comma{} and
whether another reader would take them over;
@item Programs for particular features
qpdf@comma{} veraPDF@comma{} Tesseract@comma{} ocrmypdf@comma{}
Lua@LaTeX{}@comma{} Typst and the converters@comma{} each with what it enables and how to
get it.  The worker tells the doctor which programs it runs itself: a
Nix-built worker's own qpdf@comma{} veraPDF and ocrmypdf (with the Tesseract
that comes with it) count as found even when they are not on your
@samp{PATH};
@item Integration
evil@comma{} Doom Emacs@comma{} the icon font@comma{} and this manual;
@item Directories
the directories Vellumory writes to.
@end table

Each check is marked as fine@comma{} informational@comma{} a warning or an error@comma{} with
a short explanation.  Where Emacs can fix a problem itself@comma{} such as making
Vellumory the reader for PDF files or setting up evil's keys@comma{} the report
offers a button.

In the report:

@table @asis
@item @kbd{g} (@code{vellumory-doctor})
@kindex g
@findex vellumory-doctor
Run the checks again.
@item @kbd{w} (@code{vellumory-doctor-copy})
@kindex w
@findex vellumory-doctor-copy
Copy the report as plain text@comma{} for a bug
  report.
@item @kbd{@key{TAB}}
@kindex TAB
Move to the next button; @code{<backtab>} moves back.
@end table

@findex vellumory-doctor-batch
For scripts and continuous integration@comma{} @code{vellumory-doctor-batch} prints the
same report and exits with status 1 if a check failed.  Here is part of
its report on the development machine@comma{} before @code{vellumory-install} had run:

@example
$ emacs --batch -L lisp -l vellumory-doctor -f vellumory-doctor-batch
Emacs
  ✓ Emacs 31.1  29.1 or newer is needed.
  · A graphical frame  This frame is a terminal; documents open in graphical frames.
  ✓ Image support  PBM images@comma{} as the worker sends them.
  …
Worker
  ✓ Worker program  ~/dev/emacs-pdf/.lake/build/bin/vellumory
  ✓ Worker protocol  version 3@comma{} as this Lisp expects
  ✓ Rendering engine  MuPDF 1.27.2 · 6 render threads on 12 cores
Opening PDF files
  ! PDF files open in Vellumory  They open in doc-view-mode-maybe.  `vellumory-install' makes Vellumory their reader.
  …
  · soffice  Missing: no reading office documents.  Part of LibreOffice.
@end example

A tick means fine@comma{} a dot is for information@comma{} an exclamation mark is a
warning and a cross an error.  In batch mode there is no graphical
frame@comma{} so that check is only informational there.

Two more quick checks:

@itemize
@item
In a shell@comma{} @samp{vellumory --help} lists the worker's subcommands.
@item
Open a PDF@.  The header line should soon read something like
@samp{Page 1/14   100%   Ready}.
@end itemize

If the header line says "Worker stopped" instead@comma{} or the document opens in
another mode@comma{} see @ref{Troubleshooting}.

@node Getting started
@chapter Getting started

@cindex getting started

This chapter covers what you need for everyday reading: opening a
document@comma{} finding your way around the screen@comma{} the menu that holds every
command@comma{} and the mouse.

@menu
* Opening a document::
* What you see::
* The menu on ?::
* The mouse::
* Leaving and coming back::
* Getting help::
@end menu

@node Opening a document
@section Opening a document

@cindex opening documents
@findex vellumory-mode

After @code{vellumory-install}@comma{} documents open in Vellumory wherever Emacs visits
files: with @code{C-x C-f}@comma{} from Dired@comma{} from bookmarks@comma{} from Org links.

@table @asis
@item @kbd{M-x vellumory-open}
@findex vellumory-open
Read a file in Vellumory@comma{} whatever its name.
  This works without @code{vellumory-install}.
@item @kbd{M-x vellumory-project-documents}
@findex vellumory-project-documents
Choose one of the current
  project's PDFs and open it.
@end table

When a document opens@comma{} the buffer holds no copy of the file.  The worker
reads the file itself@comma{} so even a document of hundreds of megabytes opens
without Emacs reading it into memory.  The first pages appear@comma{} and the
page previews open in a sidebar on the left (see
@code{vellumory-show-thumbnails}).

Documents can say how they want to be shown.  If a PDF asks for two
columns@comma{} it opens with two pages side by side; if it asks for its
contents@comma{} the contents sidebar opens instead of the previews.  The option
@code{vellumory-follow-document-display} turns this off.

@cindex encrypted documents
@cindex password
An encrypted document asks for its password.  If the password is wrong@comma{} it
asks again.  The password stays in memory only@comma{} so that the document can
be reloaded; it is never written anywhere.

@cindex remote files
@cindex TRAMP
A document on a remote machine (through TRAMP)@comma{} or one in a buffer without
a file@comma{} is copied into Vellumory's cache under a name made from its
content@comma{} and read from there.  You can read and search it@comma{} but editing
needs a local file.

@node What you see
@section What you see

@cindex header line
@cindex mode line

The header line above the pages shows where you are:

@example
Page 3/14   100%   Ready
@end example

That is the page in view@comma{} the number of pages@comma{} the zoom and the status.
While pages render@comma{} the status reads "Rendering…".  If the document has
page labels that differ from the page numbers@comma{} the label comes first and
the number in parentheses@comma{} as in @samp{iv (4)}.  With two pages side by side@comma{}
both are named@comma{} as in @samp{2–3/14}.  During a search the search status is
added@comma{} and the keyboard caret shows when it is active.  The mode line
shows the page as well.  To hide the header line@comma{} set
@code{vellumory-header-line} to @code{nil} (the Doom module does@comma{} because its mode line
shows the same).

Between the pages you see the "desk"@comma{} a neutral background (the face
@code{vellumory-desk}).  The sidebar on the left shows either page previews or
the document's contents; the tabs at its top switch between them.

@node The menu on ?
@section The menu on ?

@cindex command menu
@cindex transient menu
@cindex dispatch
@findex vellumory-dispatch-edit
@findex vellumory-dispatch-annotate
@findex vellumory-dispatch-export
@findex vellumory-menu

You do not have to learn many keys to start.  One key shows them all.

@table @asis
@item @kbd{?} (@code{vellumory-dispatch})
@itemx @kbd{C-c C-v}
@kindex ?
@kindex C-c C-v
@findex vellumory-dispatch
Show a menu of every Vellumory command@comma{} grouped by what
  it is for.  Press the key shown next to an entry to run it.  This is a
  transient menu@comma{} the kind Magit uses.
@end table

In a document@comma{} the menu has these groups:

@table @asis
@item Go
page@comma{} page label@comma{} heading@comma{} first and last page@comma{} back and forward.
@item Find
search for text or a regular expression@comma{} list all matches@comma{}
clear the search.
@item View
zoom; the fitted page or back to your zoom; fit the width@comma{} the
page or the content; two pages; reading mode (@code{m}); a menu of page colours
(@code{C}); night mode (@code{N}).  Zoom and night mode keep the menu open@comma{} so you
can press them repeatedly.
@item Around
page previews@comma{} contents@comma{} switching the sidebar@comma{} presenting@comma{}
reading across windows.
@item Inspect
details@comma{} validation@comma{} PDF syntax@comma{} comparing versions@comma{} the edit
history.
@item Text
selecting with the keyboard@comma{} copying@comma{} following a link.
@item More
the edit@comma{} annotation and export menus (@code{vellumory-dispatch-edit}@comma{}
@code{vellumory-dispatch-annotate} and @code{vellumory-dispatch-export})@comma{} reloading@comma{} this
manual@comma{} and the doctor that checks your setup (@code{!}).
@item Settings
turn smooth scrolling@comma{} the header line@comma{} file watching and
link previews on or off for this session@comma{} or open Customize for all
options.
@end table

The menu keys are short and belong to the menu.  The same commands have
their own keys in the reader@comma{} listed in this manual; @code{C-h k} followed by a
key@comma{} or @code{C-h f} followed by a command name@comma{} tells you more about any of
them.

In other buffers@comma{} @code{M-x vellumory-dispatch} offers what applies there:
opening a document or a virtual document@comma{} batch work on the marked files
in Dired@comma{} Org export in an Org buffer@comma{} installing or uninstalling@comma{} the
manual (@code{m})@comma{} the doctor (@code{!}) and Customize.  In reading mode@comma{} @code{?} opens a
menu of its own (see @ref{Reading mode}).

There is also a "Document" menu in the menu bar (@code{vellumory-menu}) with most
commands.

@node The mouse
@section The mouse

@cindex mouse
@cindex context menu
@cindex right-click menu

Everything the mouse does in a document:

@table @asis
@item @kbd{wheel} (@code{vellumory-wheel})
@kindex wheel
@findex vellumory-wheel
Scroll.  Trackpads scroll by their exact
  pixel deltas; a horizontal swipe or wheel scrolls sideways.
@item @kbd{C-wheel} (@code{vellumory-wheel-zoom})
@kindex C-wheel
@findex vellumory-wheel-zoom
Zoom in or out@comma{} keeping the spot
  under the pointer in place.
@item @kbd{mouse-1} (@code{vellumory-mouse-press})
@kindex mouse-1
@findex vellumory-mouse-press
On a link@comma{} follow it.  On a form
  field@comma{} fill it in.  Elsewhere@comma{} clear the selection.  Drag to select text@comma{}
  across pages if you like.
@item @kbd{double-mouse-1} (@code{vellumory-mouse-select-word})
@kindex double-mouse-1
@findex vellumory-mouse-select-word
Select a word.
@item @kbd{triple-mouse-1} (@code{vellumory-mouse-select-line})
@kindex triple-mouse-1
@findex vellumory-mouse-select-line
Select a line.
@item @kbd{C-mouse-1} (@code{vellumory-sync-backward})
@kindex C-mouse-1
@findex vellumory-sync-backward
Visit the @TeX{} source that
  produced this point (see @ref{Sync@TeX{} and AUC@TeX{}}).
@item @kbd{mouse-3} (@code{vellumory-context-menu})
@kindex mouse-3
@findex vellumory-context-menu
Show a menu of what can be done
  where you clicked.
@end table

Hovering over a link shows a hand pointer and where the link goes.  If
the mouse rests on a link within the document@comma{} a small preview of its
target opens (see @ref{Link previews}).  Hovering over an annotation shows
its text@comma{} and hovering over a form field says that a click fills it.

The right-click menu depends on the spot:

@itemize
@item
on a link@comma{} follow it@comma{} or copy its address if it leads to the web;
@item
on selected text@comma{} copy it@comma{} highlight@comma{} underline@comma{} strike out or
squiggle it@comma{} add a note@comma{} or search for it;
@item
on a page@comma{} rotate it either way@comma{} delete it@comma{} present from it@comma{} export
it@comma{} or mark it for export (and@comma{} once pages are marked@comma{} export them or
clear the marks);
@item
then always: zoom@comma{} fit@comma{} two pages@comma{} reading mode@comma{} a "Page colours"
submenu (the four colour choices as radio buttons@comma{} and night mode)@comma{} the
sidebars@comma{} details@comma{} validation@comma{} PDF syntax@comma{} the edit history@comma{} undo@comma{} redo@comma{}
and the menu of all commands.
@end itemize

The page previews@comma{} the contents and the other Vellumory buffers (the
history@comma{} the validation report and so on) have right-click menus of their
own.  If you use @code{context-menu-mode}@comma{} these entries become its menu in
Vellumory's buffers.

@node Leaving and coming back
@section Leaving and coming back

@cindex reading position
@cindex saving place
@cindex save-place-mode
@vindex vellumory-state-directory

@table @asis
@item @kbd{q}
@kindex q
Put the document away (@code{quit-window}).
@end table

Vellumory remembers exactly where you were: the page@comma{} the position on the
page and the zoom.  It saves the place whenever Emacs has been idle for
two seconds@comma{} and when you close the document.  The place is stored twice@comma{} once
for the file's name and once for its content@comma{} so a renamed or copied
document opens at the same place too.  The files are small JSON files in
@code{vellumory-state-directory} (by default @samp{~/.local/state/vellumory}).

Emacs's own @code{save-place-mode} is not used for documents: their buffer
position means nothing@comma{} and Vellumory keeps a more exact place itself.  It
drops a document's old save-place entry the next time the document is
closed.

@cindex reloading
@cindex watching files

@table @asis
@item @kbd{r} (@code{vellumory-reload})
@itemx @kbd{R}
@kindex r
@kindex R
@findex vellumory-reload
Read the file again@comma{} keeping your place.  @code{M-x revert-buffer}
  does the same.
@end table

You rarely need to reload by hand.  When the file changes on disk@comma{} for
instance because @LaTeX{} has compiled it again@comma{} Vellumory reloads it by
itself once the file has been quiet for a moment (@code{vellumory-watch-file}).
This also works when a program replaces the file by renaming a new one
over it.

@cindex saving
Saving with @code{C-x C-s} never changes the document.  The buffer holds only the
page layout@comma{} and Vellumory never writes that into your file.  Files change
only through the edits described in @ref{Editing safely}.  @code{C-x C-w} writes a
copy of the document instead (see @ref{Documents that cannot be edited}).

