There is also a "Reflowed" menu in the menu bar.  Because reading mode is
ordinary Emacs text@comma{} the rest of Emacs works on it too: @code{C-s} searches it@comma{}
the mouse and the region select it@comma{} @code{M-w} copies.  Such a search only sees
the pages loaded in the buffer.

@node Colours size and line length
@subsection Colours@comma{} size and line length

The colours come from @code{vellumory-reflow-colours}:

@table @asis
@item @code{auto}
dark while the document's pages are inverted (night mode@comma{} or
inverted with a dark theme)@comma{} your Emacs theme's colours otherwise;
@item @code{theme}
always your Emacs theme's colours;
@item @code{paper}@comma{} @code{sepia}@comma{} @code{night}
the faces @code{vellumory-reflow-paper}@comma{}
@code{vellumory-reflow-sepia} and @code{vellumory-reflow-night}.
@end table

In dark colours the pictures are inverted to match.  Lines hold at most
@code{vellumory-reflow-measure} characters (70 by default; lines of 60 to 75
characters read best)@comma{} and fewer in a narrow window; the text is centred
with margins on both sides.  The text is set in the proportional
@code{variable-pitch} font (@code{vellumory-reflow-variable-pitch})@comma{} with a little
extra space between lines (@code{vellumory-reflow-line-spacing}).  All of these
are in the customization group @code{vellumory-reflow} (see @ref{Reading mode options}).

@node Narrow windows
@subsection Narrow windows

@vindex vellumory-reflow-narrow-width
Set @code{vellumory-reflow-narrow-width} to a number of columns@comma{} and a window
narrower than that shows its document in reading mode by itself@comma{} for
example when you split the frame side by side.  Once the window is wide
enough again (eight columns more)@comma{} it shows the pages.  The default@comma{} @code{nil}@comma{}
never does this.

@node What reading mode cannot do
@subsection What reading mode cannot do

How well a page reflows depends on the document.  A tagged PDF carries
its own structure and reading order.  An untagged one reflows only as
well as the reading order MuPDF finds in its layout: columns@comma{} tables and
captions may come out in an odd order@comma{} and a heading is only recognized
by its size.  Formulas and drawings are pictures@comma{} not text@comma{} so they
cannot be searched or copied there.  Nothing can be edited from reading
mode: annotating@comma{} filling in forms and the other edits work on the
pages.

@node Presenting
@section Presenting

@cindex presentation
@cindex slides
@cindex full screen
@findex vellumory-presentation-mode
@findex vellumory-present-click
@findex vellumory-present-wheel
@findex vellumory-present-backspace
@findex vellumory-present-any-key

Vellumory can show a document full screen@comma{} one page at a time@comma{} like the
presentation views of Evince and Okular.

@table @asis
@item @kbd{C-c C-p} (@code{vellumory-present})
@kindex C-c C-p
@findex vellumory-present
Present the document from the
  current page.  With a prefix argument@comma{} start at the first page.  The
  right-click menu on a page offers "Present from here".
@end table

The presentation gets a frame of its own@comma{} on another monitor if there is
one (a projector@comma{} say; see @code{vellumory-present-monitor}).  Each page is fitted
to the screen@comma{} centred on black (@code{vellumory-present-background})@comma{} and
rendered sharp at the screen's resolution.  The pages on either side are
rendered ahead@comma{} so changing pages is immediate.

In the presentation:

@table @asis
@item @kbd{@key{SPC}} (@code{vellumory-present-next})
@itemx @kbd{<right>}
@itemx @kbd{<down>}
@itemx @kbd{<next>}
@itemx @kbd{n}
@kindex SPC
@kindex <right>
@kindex <down>
@kindex <next>
@kindex n
@findex vellumory-present-next
Next page.  A left click and the wheel also go forward
  (@code{vellumory-present-click}@comma{} @code{vellumory-present-wheel}); so do @code{j}@comma{} @code{l}@comma{}
  @code{C-n} and @code{C-v}.  Past the last page comes an end screen@comma{} and the next
  press leaves.
@item @kbd{S-@key{SPC}} (@code{vellumory-present-previous})
@itemx @kbd{<left>}
@itemx @kbd{<up>}
@itemx @kbd{<prior>}
@itemx @kbd{p}
@kindex S-SPC
@kindex <left>
@kindex <up>
@kindex <prior>
@kindex p
@findex vellumory-present-previous
Previous page.  A right click and the wheel also go back; so do
  @code{h}@comma{} @code{k}@comma{} @code{C-p} and @code{M-v}.
@item @kbd{<home>} (@code{vellumory-present-first})
@itemx @kbd{g}
@kindex <home>
@kindex g
@findex vellumory-present-first
First page (also @code{<} and @code{M-<}).
@item @kbd{<end>} (@code{vellumory-present-last})
@itemx @kbd{G}
@kindex <end>
@kindex G
@findex vellumory-present-last
Last page (also @code{>} and @code{M->}).
@item @kbd{0} (@code{vellumory-present-digit})
@itemx @kbd{@key{RET}} (@code{vellumory-present-return})
@kindex 0
@kindex RET
@findex vellumory-present-digit
@findex vellumory-present-return
Type a page number@comma{} then @code{RET} to go
  there.  @code{DEL} (@code{vellumory-present-backspace}) deletes a digit@comma{} or goes back a
  page when you are not typing one.  @code{RET} alone goes forward.
@item @kbd{b} (@code{vellumory-present-black})
@itemx @kbd{.}
@kindex b
@kindex .
@findex vellumory-present-black
Blank the screen to black; again@comma{} or any key@comma{} returns
  (@code{vellumory-present-any-key}).
@item @kbd{w} (@code{vellumory-present-white})
@itemx @kbd{@comma{}}
@kindex w
@kindex @comma{}
@findex vellumory-present-white
Blank the screen to white.
@item @kbd{q} (@code{vellumory-present-quit})
@itemx @kbd{<escape>}
@kindex q
@kindex <escape>
@findex vellumory-present-quit
Leave the presentation (also @code{Q} and @code{C-g}).  The reader
  then shows the page last presented.
@end table

Links on a page can be clicked.  A page that sets a display time (/Dur)
advances by itself after that time (@code{vellumory-present-follow-durations}).
The mouse wheel turns at most one page per 0.3 seconds@comma{} so a trackpad's
stream of events does not race through the slides.

@cindex transitions
Page transitions that a document defines (/Trans) are drawn as it asks
(@code{vellumory-present-transitions}).  Wipe@comma{} Push@comma{} Cover@comma{} Uncover@comma{} Split@comma{} Box and
Blinds move the two page images@comma{} with their directions and durations.
Dissolve@comma{} Glitter@comma{} Fade and Fly are composed by the worker from both pages@comma{}
frame by frame.  On the test machine that reached about 60 frames a second
at 1278 × 958 pixels.  Other styles change the page at once@comma{} and a key
pressed during a transition finishes it.  On an Emacs with canvas images
(Emacs 32 since August 2026)@comma{} composed frames replace one image's pixels
in place@comma{} which costs Emacs less time per frame (@code{vellumory-present-canvas}).

@node Reading across windows
@section Reading across windows

@cindex follow mode
@cindex columns

On a wide screen you can read a document in columns@comma{} as @code{follow-mode} does
for text: each window starts exactly where the one before it ends.

@table @asis
@item @kbd{M-x vellumory-follow-split}
@findex vellumory-follow-split
Show the document in side-by-side
  windows that follow each other@comma{} two by default; a numeric prefix sets the
  number of columns.  It is on @code{F} in the command menu.
@item @kbd{M-x vellumory-follow-mode}
@findex vellumory-follow-mode
Turn the following on or off for the
  windows that already show the document in this frame.
@end table

The windows are chained in follow-mode's order: top to bottom@comma{} then left
to right.  Scrolling any of them moves all of them@comma{} and a screen is all of
them together.  Unlike @code{follow-mode}@comma{} which chains windows by buffer
positions@comma{} Vellumory chains them by pixels@comma{} so a page can continue from
the foot of one column to the head of the next.  Each window's header line
names its own page.  If you turn on @code{follow-mode} in a document@comma{} Vellumory
turns on its own mode instead.

@node Other formats
@section Other formats

@cindex formats
@cindex EPUB
@cindex XPS
@cindex CBZ
@cindex FB2

MuPDF reads several formats besides PDF@comma{} and so does Vellumory: XPS and
OpenXPS@comma{} CBZ comic book archives@comma{} and the e-book formats EPUB and FB2.
@code{vellumory-file-extensions} lists the extensions that @code{vellumory-install}
associates.

EPUB and FB2 books have no fixed pages.  Vellumory lays them out on pages
of @code{vellumory-reflow-page-size} points (450 × 600 by default)@comma{} with text of
@code{vellumory-reflow-text-size} points (11 by default)@comma{} and applies the CSS file
@code{vellumory-reflow-stylesheet} if you set one.  The text-scale keys change the
size for the book in view (see @ref{Zoom and fit}).  Each layout is a document
of its own as far as rendering and saved places are concerned@comma{} so nothing
from one size leaks into another.

@menu
* Converted formats::
@end menu

@node Converted formats
@subsection Converted formats

@cindex DVI
@cindex PostScript
@cindex DjVu
@cindex office documents
@cindex conversion

Like DocView@comma{} Vellumory reads some formats by converting them to PDF:

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Format
@tab Converter
@item DVI
@tab dvipdfmx
@item PostScript and EPS
@tab ps2pdf (Ghostscript)
@item DjVu
@tab ddjvu (DjVuLibre)
@item OpenDocument@comma{} Word@comma{} Excel@comma{} PowerPoint@comma{} RTF
@tab soffice (LibreOffice)
@end multitable

@code{vellumory-converters} holds these rules@comma{} and you can change them.  A format
is associated only when its converter is installed.  PostScript is left
to @code{ps-mode}@comma{} where it is usually edited: read a PostScript file with @code{M-x
vellumory-converted-mode} (@code{vellumory-converted-not-visited} lists the formats
treated this way).

@table @asis
@item @kbd{M-x vellumory-converted-mode}
@findex vellumory-converted-mode
Read the file in this buffer
  converted to PDF@.
@end table

The conversion is cached under @code{vellumory-cache-directory}@comma{} keyed by the
original's content and the converter@comma{} so each version converts only once.
When the original changes@comma{} for example a DVI that @TeX{} has written again@comma{}
it is converted again and shown at the same place.  While a conversion
runs@comma{} the buffer says so.  Converted documents can be read@comma{} searched and
selected like any PDF@comma{} but not edited: an edit would not reach the
original.  Leaving the mode shows the original file again.  The
LibreOffice route is not covered by the project's tests.

@node Virtual documents
@section Virtual documents

@cindex virtual documents
@cindex vpdf files
@findex vellumory-virtual-mode
@findex vellumory-virtual-view-mode
@findex vellumory-virtual-edit-mode

A virtual document lists pages@comma{} or parts of pages@comma{} of other documents@comma{} in
the order you want to read them.  Vellumory reads the format of pdf-tools'
@samp{.vpdf} files@comma{} so files written for pdf-tools open unchanged:

@lisp
;; %VPDF 1.0
(("paper.pdf" (1 . 4) 7)                ; pages 1–4 and 7
 ("slides.pdf")                         ; every page
 ("scan.pdf" (2 0 0 1 0.5)))            ; the top half of page 2
@end lisp

A page specification is a page number@comma{} a range @code{(FIRST . LAST)} (where a
@code{LAST} of 0 means the last page)@comma{} or either followed by a region @code{LEFT TOP
RIGHT BOTTOM} in fractions of the page as shown; @code{(nil . REGION)} means every
page.  A file that begins with the line @samp{;; %VPDF 1.0} opens as a virtual
document.

Vellumory composes the pages into one real PDF with MuPDF@comma{} cached by the
list and by every source's content@comma{} and reads that.  So everything works
on it: search@comma{} occur@comma{} selection@comma{} links@comma{} saved places.  The header line
names each page's source.

@table @asis
@item @kbd{M-x vellumory-virtual-create}
@findex vellumory-virtual-create
Start a new list: in Dired@comma{} of the
  marked files (@code{; V}); elsewhere@comma{} of every PDF in the directory.  The list
  opens for editing; @code{C-c C-c} reads it.  Save it as a @samp{.vpdf} file to keep
  it.
@item @kbd{C-c C-c} (@code{vellumory-virtual-edit})
@kindex C-c C-c
@findex vellumory-virtual-edit
In a virtual document@comma{} edit its
  list; @code{C-c C-c} (@code{vellumory-virtual-mode}) shows it again.
@item @kbd{M-@}} (@code{vellumory-virtual-next-document})
@itemx @kbd{M-@{} (@code{vellumory-virtual-previous-document})
@kindex M-@}
@kindex M-@{
@findex vellumory-virtual-next-document
@findex vellumory-virtual-previous-document
Go to the first page of
  the next or previous source.
@end table

A virtual document cannot be edited as a whole; edit its sources instead.

@node How pages are rendered
@section How pages are rendered

@cindex rendering
@cindex previews while scrolling

You never need to think about rendering@comma{} but knowing a little explains
what you see.  Each page is laid out as strips of images.  Only the strips
that a window shows are rendered at full resolution@comma{} at your screen's
pixel density@comma{} on several threads (by default the processor's cores
less two@comma{} at least two and at most six).  Work for strips that scroll out of
view is cancelled.  While you move faster than pages can be rendered@comma{} a
quick low-resolution preview of the whole page fills in@comma{} and it sharpens
about a tenth of a second after you stop (@code{vellumory-refine-delay}).  Pages
that are cheap to draw are rendered sharp at once.  When Emacs is idle@comma{} a
screen ahead and half a screen behind are prepared.

Rendered strips are kept in a private directory under @samp{XDG_RUNTIME_DIR}
(normally in memory) and removed when the worker exits.  Page previews and
sidebar thumbnails are also kept across sessions in
@samp{~/.cache/vellumory/rasters}@comma{} so a document you open again shows its
previews at once.

@node Finding your way
@chapter Finding your way

@cindex navigation

This chapter is about getting to the place you want: searching the text@comma{}
listing every match@comma{} using the document's contents and page labels@comma{} page
previews@comma{} links@comma{} and returning to where you were.

