Go to a heading of the contents@comma{} with
  completion.
@end table

Imenu knows the contents too@comma{} so @code{M-x imenu} and @code{consult-imenu} jump to
headings.  In the contents sidebar:

@table @asis
@item @kbd{@key{RET}} (@code{vellumory-outline-activate})
@kindex RET
@findex vellumory-outline-activate
Go to the heading on this line.
@item @kbd{@key{TAB}}
@kindex TAB
Fold or unfold a heading.
@item @kbd{<backtab>}
@kindex <backtab>
Show everything.
@item @kbd{o} (@code{vellumory-outline-return})
@kindex o
@findex vellumory-outline-return
Return to the document.
@item @kbd{s}
@kindex s
Show the page previews in its place.
@item @kbd{q}
@kindex q
Close the sidebar.
@end table

If a document has no contents@comma{} the sidebar says so@comma{} @code{i} tells you@comma{} and the
Contents tab at the top of the sidebar is greyed out.

@node Page labels
@section Page labels

@cindex page labels

Many books number their front matter in roman numerals@comma{} or chapters as
"A-1".  Vellumory names pages by these labels everywhere: in the header
line@comma{} in the previews@comma{} in occur and in Org links.  Where a label differs
from the page number@comma{} both are shown@comma{} as in @samp{iv (4)}.  @code{L} goes to a page by
its label (see @ref{Moving by page}).

@node Page previews
@section Page previews

@cindex page previews
@cindex thumbnails
@cindex sidebar
@findex vellumory-thumbnails-mode

@table @asis
@item @kbd{T} (@code{vellumory-thumbnails})
@kindex T
@findex vellumory-thumbnails
Show or hide page previews in the left
  sidebar.
@item @kbd{s} (@code{vellumory-sidebar-switch})
@kindex s
@findex vellumory-sidebar-switch
Switch the sidebar between the page
  previews and the contents.  With no sidebar@comma{} show the previews.  A click
  on the "Pages" or "Contents" tab at the top of the sidebar does the same.
@end table

The previews open by default (@code{vellumory-show-thumbnails}).  Every page gets a
placeholder of its own shape with its label underneath; only the previews
in view are rendered@comma{} at most two at a time and after the pages you are
reading.  The page you are reading is highlighted and kept in view.  In
the previews:

@table @asis
@item @kbd{@key{RET}} (@code{vellumory-thumbnail-visit})
@kindex RET
@findex vellumory-thumbnail-visit
Go to this page (so does a click).
@item @kbd{n} (@code{vellumory-thumbnail-next})
@itemx @kbd{j}
@kindex n
@kindex j
@findex vellumory-thumbnail-next
Next preview.
@item @kbd{p} (@code{vellumory-thumbnail-previous})
@itemx @kbd{k}
@kindex p
@kindex k
@findex vellumory-thumbnail-previous
Previous preview.
@item @kbd{T}
@kindex T
Close the sidebar (so does @code{q}).
@end table

The size of the previews is @code{vellumory-thumbnail-width} by
@code{vellumory-thumbnail-height}.  The sidebar closes by itself when its
document leaves the frame.

@node Links
@section Links

@cindex links
@cindex link hints

Links work as you would expect: hover to see where one goes@comma{} click to
follow it.  Links within the document jump there; links to web pages open
in your browser after asking (@code{vellumory-confirm-external-links}).

@table @asis
@item @kbd{f} (@code{vellumory-follow-link})
@kindex f
@findex vellumory-follow-link
Label every visible link with a short
  code and follow the one you choose.  The codes are made from the letters
  in @code{vellumory-link-hint-keys}.
@end table

@node Link previews
@section Link previews

@cindex link preview
@cindex hover preview
@vindex vellumory-link-preview
@vindex vellumory-link-preview-delay
@vindex vellumory-link-preview-size
@findex vellumory-link-preview-hide
@findex vellumory-link-preview-scroll-up

A reference such as "see Figure 3" or "[12]" sends you elsewhere in the
document.  Rest the mouse on such a link@comma{} and after a moment
(@code{vellumory-link-preview-delay}@comma{} 0.3 seconds@comma{} as in GNOME's Papers) a small
frame opens beside it and shows where the link leads@comma{} without leaving
your place.

The preview shows a strip of the target page at the zoom and in the page
colours you read in@comma{} lined up over the page's own columns@comma{} with the
target about a third of the way down.  Its header names the page.  It
opens above the link when there is room@comma{} otherwise below it.  Its size is
@code{vellumory-link-preview-size}: a fraction of the window's width and height@comma{}
92% by 35% by default.

@table @asis
@item @kbd{wheel} (@code{vellumory-link-preview-scroll-down})
@kindex wheel
@findex vellumory-link-preview-scroll-down
In the preview@comma{} the
  wheel scrolls it (@code{vellumory-link-preview-scroll-up} scrolls back)@comma{} so you
  can read on past its first lines.
@item @kbd{mouse-1} (@code{vellumory-link-preview-visit})
@kindex mouse-1
@findex vellumory-link-preview-visit
In the preview@comma{} go to the
  link's target.  @code{B} (@code{vellumory-history-back}) returns to where you were.
@end table

Moving the mouse away from both the link and the preview closes it@comma{} and
so does any key.  Only links within the document are previewed; links to
web pages are not.  Previews need a graphical display@comma{} since they are
child frames.  @code{vellumory-link-preview} set to @code{nil} turns them off@comma{} and @code{;l} in
the command menu toggles them for the session.

@node Going back
@section Going back

@cindex history of positions
@cindex back and forward

After following a link you will often want to return.

@table @asis
@item @kbd{B} (@code{vellumory-history-back})
@kindex B
@findex vellumory-history-back
Return to where you were before the
  last link or jump.
@item @kbd{N} (@code{vellumory-history-forward})
@kindex N
@findex vellumory-history-forward
Undo that.
@end table

These are the keys pdf-tools uses too.  They are also @code{[} and @code{]} in the
command menu@comma{} @code{C-o} and @code{C-i} with evil@comma{} and @code{[} and @code{]} on Doom's localleader.

Following a link within the document (also from its preview)@comma{} an Org
link@comma{} a register@comma{} a Sync@TeX{} forward search or an entry of the annotation
list each remember where you were.  Going to a page with @code{g} does not.

@node Bookmarks and registers
@section Bookmarks and registers

@cindex bookmarks
@cindex registers

Emacs bookmarks remember the exact place in a document: the page and the
position on it.

@table @asis
@item @kbd{C-x r m}
@kindex C-x r m
Set a bookmark here (@code{bookmark-set}).  Its default name is
  the buffer and the page@comma{} such as "paper.pdf@comma{} page 7".
@item @kbd{C-x r b}
@kindex C-x r b
Jump to a bookmark (@code{bookmark-jump}); the document opens at
  the bookmarked place@comma{} even if it was closed.
@end table

Registers hold exact places too.  With evil@comma{} @code{m} followed by a letter stores
the place and @code{'} or @code{`} followed by the letter returns there.  The commands
are @code{vellumory-point-to-register} and @code{vellumory-jump-to-register}@comma{} and Emacs's
own @code{C-x r j} also jumps to such a register.  They live in
@samp{vellumory-evil.el}.  That file does not need evil@comma{} so without evil@comma{} load
it with @code{(require 'vellumory-evil)} and run the commands with @code{M-x}@comma{} or bind
them to keys of your own.
@findex vellumory-point-to-register
@findex vellumory-jump-to-register

