The command line prints the same report as JSON with @code{vellumory info FILE}.

@node Signatures
@section Signatures

@cindex digital signatures
@cindex signatures, verifying

The details report verifies every digital signature itself@comma{} with OpenSSL@.
For each one it tells you:

@itemize
@item
whether the signed bytes are intact;
@item
who signed (the name in the certificate);
@item
whether the signer's certificate chains to the certificates your system
trusts@comma{} and if not@comma{} why not (a self-signed certificate@comma{} for example);
@item
whether the signature covers the whole file@comma{} or the document was changed
after signing.
@end itemize

Because every edit rewrites the file@comma{} an edit invalidates signatures.
Before editing a signed document@comma{} Vellumory asks.  The signed version stays
in the history@comma{} and undo restores it byte for byte@comma{} signatures intact.

Vellumory verifies signatures; it cannot create them.  @code{vellumory signatures
FILE} prints the same verification as JSON@.

@node Validating PDF/A PDF/UA and WTPDF
@section Validating PDF/A@comma{} PDF/UA and WTPDF

@cindex validation
@cindex veraPDF
@cindex PDF/A
@cindex PDF/UA
@cindex WTPDF
@cindex accessibility
@findex vellumory-validation-mode

PDF/A is the ISO standard for archiving@comma{} PDF/UA the one for accessibility@comma{}
and WTPDF (Well-Tagged PDF) its counterpart from the PDF Association.
veraPDF is the reference validator for them.  Vellumory runs it for you.

@table @asis
@item @kbd{V} (@code{vellumory-validate})
@kindex V
@findex vellumory-validate
Validate the document against a profile you
  choose@comma{} and show the report in @samp{*Validation: NAME*}.
@end table

The profiles are: "auto" (whatever the document claims)@comma{} PDF/A-1a@comma{} 1b@comma{}
2a@comma{} 2b@comma{} 2u@comma{} 3a@comma{} 3b@comma{} 3u@comma{} 4@comma{} 4e and 4f@comma{} PDF/UA-1 and PDF/UA-2@comma{} and WTPDF 1.0
for reuse and for accessibility.

The report lists the failed rules by specification and clause@comma{} each with
its failed checks.  The same check failing hundreds of times on a page is
one line with a count.  A check that names a page takes you there with
@code{RET}@comma{} and @code{M-g n} (@code{next-error}) walks through the checks from anywhere.

@table @asis
@item @kbd{n} (@code{vellumory-validation-next-rule})
@kindex n
@findex vellumory-validation-next-rule
Next failed rule.
@item @kbd{p} (@code{vellumory-validation-previous-rule})
@kindex p
@findex vellumory-validation-previous-rule
Previous failed rule.
@item @kbd{b} (@code{vellumory-validation-beyond})
@kindex b
@findex vellumory-validation-beyond
Go to the checks beyond veraPDF
  (next section).
@item @kbd{@key{TAB}}
@kindex TAB
Next button; @code{<backtab>}@comma{} the previous one.
@end table

The verdict is veraPDF's.  PDF/UA has requirements that only a person can
judge@comma{} such as whether alternative text is meaningful or the reading order
makes sense@comma{} and the report says so.  Validation needs veraPDF@comma{} a Java
program@comma{} and a document read from a local file.

@node Checks beyond veraPDF
@section Checks beyond veraPDF

@cindex preflight

Some problems matter whatever a document claims to be.  The validation
report shows them below veraPDF's results@comma{} and they appear at once@comma{}
because they take milliseconds.  On the command line@comma{} @code{vellumory preflight
FILE} prints them as JSON@.

Errors:
@itemize
@item
a damaged cross-reference table (readers repair it differently);
@item
links whose destination is missing or outside the document (MuPDF would
silently send them to page 1);
@item
contents entries that lead nowhere;
@item
40-bit encryption.
@end itemize

Warnings:
@itemize
@item
RC4 encryption@comma{} which can be broken;
@item
earlier versions of the content still inside the file after incremental
updates@comma{} and recoverable by anyone (not reported for signed documents@comma{}
where incremental updates are expected);
@item
JavaScript;
@item
an XFA form;
@item
fonts that are not embedded;
@item
crop@comma{} trim@comma{} bleed or art boxes larger than the page.
@end itemize

Notes:
@itemize
@item
composite fonts without a Unicode map@comma{} whose text cannot be copied or
searched reliably;
@item
no title@comma{} no document language@comma{} no tagging;
@item
larger than 10 MB and not linearized;
@item
pages of different sizes.
@end itemize

Where Vellumory can fix a problem@comma{} the message names the key: @code{e c}
rewrites the file (repairing it and removing earlier versions)@comma{} @code{e E}
encrypts with AES-256@comma{} @code{e i} sets the title and language@comma{} @code{e L} linearizes.

