@node Editing safely
@chapter Editing safely

@cindex editing
@cindex safety

You can change documents in Vellumory without fear.  This is the promise:
no edit can lose an earlier version@comma{} and any state the document has been
in can be brought back exactly.

@menu
* How every edit is kept::
* Page edits::
* Document edits::
* Compression and encryption::
* Undo and redo::
* The history tree::
* Where the history lives::
* Documents that cannot be edited::
* Writing new files::
@end menu

@node How every edit is kept
@section How every edit is kept

@cindex snapshots
@cindex content-addressed store
@cindex atomic replacement

Every edit goes the same way:

@enumerate
@item
It works on an unchangeable snapshot of the document@comma{} taken from a
store in which each version is filed under the SHA-256 hash of its
bytes.
@item
The result is checked: it must open@comma{} and it must have pages.
@item
The result is stored@comma{} and the edit is recorded in the document's
history@comma{} with the time and a description.
@item
Only then is your file replaced@comma{} in one step (a new file is written
beside it and renamed over it)@comma{} keeping its permissions.
@end enumerate

Nothing in the store is ever deleted@comma{} and identical versions are stored
once.  Before restoring a snapshot@comma{} Vellumory checks it against its hash.
If the file was changed by another program since Vellumory last saw it@comma{}
that version is recorded first@comma{} as "changed outside Vellumory"@comma{} so it is
never lost either.  Edits are deterministic: the same edit of the same
bytes gives the same result.  Two Emacs sessions@comma{} or Emacs and a script@comma{}
can edit the same document; their edits are taken one at a time.

After an edit the message names the new state@comma{} for example
@samp{rotate pages 1 by +90° (14 pages) · state 1}@comma{} and how to undo it.

@cindex PDF/A, keeping
Edits keep archival documents archival.  Every rewrite puts the end of
line before each stream's end that PDF/A demands@comma{} and documents claiming
PDF/A-1 never get object streams@comma{} which PDF/A-1 forbids.  The test suite
checks with veraPDF that rotating@comma{} keeping pages@comma{} page labels@comma{} compression
and metadata edits leave PDF/A-1b and PDF/A-2b documents compliant.

All edit keys start with @code{e}@comma{} and @code{e} in the command menu shows them grouped.

