@node Document edits
@section Document edits

@cindex metadata, editing
@cindex title
@cindex document language
@cindex attachments
@cindex associated files
@cindex stamps
@cindex watermarks
@cindex OCR
@cindex text recognition
@findex vellumory-metadata-mode
@findex vellumory-metadata-commit
@findex vellumory-metadata-cancel

@table @asis
@item @kbd{e i} (@code{vellumory-edit-metadata})
@kindex e i
@findex vellumory-edit-metadata
Edit the title@comma{} author@comma{} subject@comma{}
  keywords@comma{} creator@comma{} producer@comma{} language@comma{} whether viewers show the title
  instead of the file name@comma{} and the creation date@comma{} in a form buffer
  @samp{*Metadata: NAME*}.
@end table

In the metadata form@comma{} @code{C-c C-c} (@code{vellumory-metadata-commit}) records the
changes as one edit@comma{} and @code{C-c C-k} (@code{vellumory-metadata-cancel}) discards
them.  Emptying a field removes it.  The modification date becomes the
time of the edit.  Vellumory keeps the three places where PDF stores
metadata consistent@comma{} as PDF/A and PDF/UA require: the Info dictionary@comma{} the
document catalog (language and title display) and the XMP packet@comma{} which is
updated with Adobe's XMP Toolkit (through Exempi) so that every other XMP
property@comma{} including the PDF/A and PDF/UA identification@comma{} stays.  PDF 2.0
documents get no Info dictionary added.

@table @asis
@item @kbd{e A} (@code{vellumory-attach-file})
@kindex e A
@findex vellumory-attach-file
Embed a file in the document@comma{} under a
  name and with a description.  With a prefix argument@comma{} also say how it
  relates to the document (Source@comma{} Data@comma{} Alternative@comma{} Supplement@comma{}
  EncryptedPayload@comma{} FormData@comma{} Schema or Unspecified)@comma{} which makes it an
  associated file in the sense of PDF 2.0 and PDF/A-3.
@item @kbd{e D} (@code{vellumory-remove-attachment})
@kindex e D
@findex vellumory-remove-attachment
Remove an embedded file by its
  name.
@end table

Attachments follow the standards.  In PDF/A-3 and PDF/A-4 documents every
attachment becomes an associated file@comma{} with the relationship Unspecified
unless you name one.  PDF/A-1 forbids attachments@comma{} and PDF/A-2 and plain
PDF/A-4 forbid attachments that are not PDF/A themselves; Vellumory refuses
those instead of silently breaking the document's claim.  A replaced or
removed attachment leaves nothing behind in the file.

@table @asis
@item @kbd{e w} (@code{vellumory-stamp})
@kindex e w
@findex vellumory-stamp
Draw the first page of another PDF over the
  pages you name (a qpdf page range; @samp{1-z} is every page).  With a prefix
  argument@comma{} draw it beneath them instead@comma{} as a letterhead or watermark
  behind the text.
@item @kbd{e o} (@code{vellumory-ocr})
@kindex e o
@findex vellumory-ocr
Recognize text with ocrmypdf@comma{} adding an
  invisible@comma{} searchable text layer.  You give the Tesseract languages
  (@samp{eng}@comma{} or @samp{deu+eng} for two) and what to do with pages that already have
  text: @samp{skip} them@comma{} @samp{redo} an earlier OCR layer@comma{} or @samp{force} recognition by
  rasterizing every page first.  This can take a while.
@item @kbd{e f}
@kindex e f
Fill in the form (see @ref{Filling in forms}).
@end table

@node Compression and encryption
@section Compression and encryption

@cindex compression
@cindex linearization
@cindex encryption

@table @asis
@item @kbd{e c} (@code{vellumory-compress})
@kindex e c
@findex vellumory-compress
Compress losslessly: object streams@comma{} and
  every zlib-compressed stream recompressed at the strongest level.  The
  history shows the size before and after.  Rewriting the file this way also repairs a damaged structure and
  removes earlier versions left by incremental updates.
@item @kbd{e L} (@code{vellumory-linearize})
@kindex e L
@findex vellumory-linearize
Linearize the document ("fast web view")@comma{}
  so that a browser can show the first page before the whole file has
  arrived.
@item @kbd{e E} (@code{vellumory-encrypt})
@kindex e E
@findex vellumory-encrypt
Encrypt with AES-256.  You give a password
  to open the document (empty for none)@comma{} a password to change its
  permissions@comma{} and the permissions: printing (full@comma{} low resolution or
  none)@comma{} changes (all@comma{} annotations only@comma{} forms only@comma{} assembly only@comma{} or
  none)@comma{} and whether text and images may be copied.
@item @kbd{e P} (@code{vellumory-decrypt})
@kindex e P
@findex vellumory-decrypt
Remove the encryption@comma{} given the password.
@end table

Passwords never appear on a command line@comma{} where other users of the machine
could see them@comma{} and are never recorded in the history.  The reader keeps
the password of the document you encrypted in memory@comma{} so it can show the
result.

@node Undo and redo
@section Undo and redo

@cindex undo
@cindex redo

@table @asis
@item @kbd{C-/} (@code{vellumory-undo})
@itemx @kbd{C-x u}
@itemx @kbd{e u}
@kindex C-/
@kindex C-x u
@kindex e u
@findex vellumory-undo
Go back to the state before the last edit.  Emacs's undo keys
  run this command in documents.
@item @kbd{C-?} (@code{vellumory-redo})
@itemx @kbd{e U}
@kindex C-?
@kindex e U
@findex vellumory-redo
Return to the most recent state after the current one.
@end table

Undo and redo rewrite the file from the stored snapshots@comma{} byte for byte.
They work across Emacs sessions: the history is stored with the document's
snapshots@comma{} not in Emacs's memory.

@node The history tree
@section The history tree

@cindex history tree
@cindex branches
@cindex checkout
@findex vellumory-history-mode

When you undo and then make a different edit@comma{} the old line of edits is not
thrown away; the history branches@comma{} like a version control system.

@table @asis
@item @kbd{e h} (@code{vellumory-history})
@kindex e h
@findex vellumory-history
Show every state of the document as a tree@comma{}
  in @samp{*History: NAME*}.
@end table

It looks like this (here after rotating page 1@comma{} undoing that@comma{} and then
keeping only the first three pages):

@example
~/papers/paper.pdf

○ 2026-10-29 22:01:15  opened (14 pages)  3662ff519e
├─ ○ 2026-10-29 22:01:15  rotate pages 1 by +90° (14 pages)  a428dc9047
└─ ● 2026-10-29 22:01:15  keep pages 1@comma{}2@comma{}3 (3 pages)  1548acec89

c make current  v view without changing the file  d compare with now  g refresh  q quit
@end example

The filled circle is the current state.  Each line shows when the state
was recorded@comma{} what the edit was@comma{} and the beginning of the snapshot's hash.

@table @asis
@item @kbd{@key{RET}} (@code{vellumory-history-checkout})
@itemx @kbd{c}
@kindex RET
@kindex c
@findex vellumory-history-checkout
Make the state on this line current.  The file is rewritten from
  its snapshot@comma{} and the reader shows it.  Nothing is lost: the state you
  left stays in the tree.
@item @kbd{v} (@code{vellumory-history-view})
@kindex v
@findex vellumory-history-view
Open the state on this line in a reader
  of its own@comma{} read-only@comma{} without changing the file.  Its buffer is named
  after the file and the state@comma{} as in @samp{paper.pdf@@1}.
@item @kbd{d} (@code{vellumory-history-diff})
@kindex d
@findex vellumory-history-diff
Compare the state on this line with the
  current file (see @ref{Comparing versions}).
@item @kbd{n} (@code{vellumory-history-next})
@itemx @kbd{j}
@kindex n
@kindex j
@findex vellumory-history-next
Next state.
@item @kbd{p} (@code{vellumory-history-previous})
@itemx @kbd{k}
@kindex p
@kindex k
@findex vellumory-history-previous
Previous state.
@item @kbd{g} (@code{vellumory-history-refresh})
@kindex g
@findex vellumory-history-refresh
Read the history again.
@end table

@node Where the history lives
@section Where the history lives

@cindex store location
@cindex VELLUMORY_STORE

The snapshots and histories are kept in @samp{$XDG_DATA_HOME/vellumory}@comma{} which is
usually @samp{~/.local/share/vellumory}.  This is durable data@comma{} not a cache:
@samp{objects/} holds the snapshots@comma{} @samp{history/} one small JSON file per document@comma{}
and @samp{locks/} the files that keep two editors from interfering.  The
environment variable @samp{VELLUMORY_STORE} moves the whole store elsewhere@comma{} which
is useful for experiments.

Two things are good to know:

@itemize
@item
A document's history belongs to its path.  If you move or rename the
file@comma{} its next edit starts a new history for the new path.  The old
snapshots stay in the store@comma{} but the new history does not link to them.
@item
The store only grows: every state is a complete copy of the document@comma{}
stored once.  There is no command to prune it yet.  Its files are ordinary
files; deleting them is possible@comma{} but it takes away the ability to go
back.
@end itemize

@node Documents that cannot be edited
@section Documents that cannot be edited

@cindex read-only files

Some documents are not edited@comma{} and Vellumory tells you why:

@itemize
@item
A read-only file@comma{} or a file in a read-only directory: edits replace the
file@comma{} so they need write access.  Write a copy and edit that (see
below).  Reading@comma{} searching@comma{} inspecting and exporting work as usual.
@item
A converted document (DVI@comma{} PostScript@comma{} DjVu@comma{} office): the edit would not
reach the original.
@item
A virtual document: edit its sources.
@item
A document that is not a local file (remote@comma{} or in a buffer of its own).
@item
A snapshot opened from the history tree with @code{v}.
@end itemize

@cindex copy of a document

@table @asis
@item @kbd{C-x C-w} (@code{vellumory-write-copy})
@kindex C-x C-w
@findex vellumory-write-copy
Write a copy of the document to a
  file you name@comma{} make the copy writable@comma{} and visit it@comma{} so that you can
  edit the copy.  The suggested name is the document's with @samp{-copy} added.
  In a reader@comma{} @code{C-x C-w} runs this command instead of @code{write-file}.
@end table

The copy is of the PDF that Vellumory reads@comma{} so a converted@comma{} virtual or
remote document becomes an ordinary PDF file of its own@comma{} which can be
edited.

A signed document can be edited@comma{} but Vellumory asks first@comma{} because the edit
invalidates its signatures (see @ref{Signatures}).

@node Writing new files
@section Writing new files

@cindex extracting pages
@cindex splitting documents
@cindex exporting text
@cindex marked pages

These commands write new files and leave the document as it is.  They
work on read-only documents too.

@table @asis
@item @kbd{e x} (@code{vellumory-extract-pages})
@kindex e x
@findex vellumory-extract-pages
Write the pages you name (a qpdf
  page range such as @samp{1-3@comma{}7}) to a new document.  The outline and the
  structure tags of those pages are kept.
@item @kbd{e s} (@code{vellumory-split})
@kindex e s
@findex vellumory-split
Split the document into documents of N pages
  each@comma{} written into a directory you choose@comma{} named after the document and
  the page range.
@item @kbd{e t} (@code{vellumory-export-text})
@kindex e t
@findex vellumory-export-text
Write the text of every page to a
  file@comma{} with a form feed between pages.
@item @kbd{M-x vellumory-export-page}
@findex vellumory-export-page
Write the page in view to a new
  document.  The suggested name is the document's name with the page
  number@comma{} beside it.
@end table

@cindex marking pages for export
You can also collect pages first and export them together:

@table @asis
@item @kbd{M-x vellumory-mark-page}
@findex vellumory-mark-page
Mark the page in view for export@comma{} or unmark
  it.
@item @kbd{M-x vellumory-export-marked-pages}
@findex vellumory-export-marked-pages
Write the marked pages@comma{} in page
  order@comma{} to a new document.
@item @kbd{M-x vellumory-unmark-all}
@findex vellumory-unmark-all
Clear every mark.
@end table

In the page previews@comma{} marking works as in Dired@comma{} and marked previews show
a check mark:

@table @asis
@item @kbd{m} (@code{vellumory-thumbnail-mark-page})
@kindex m
@findex vellumory-thumbnail-mark-page
Mark the page at point and move
  to the next.
@item @kbd{u} (@code{vellumory-thumbnail-unmark-page})
@kindex u
@findex vellumory-thumbnail-unmark-page
Unmark it and move to the next.
@item @kbd{U} (@code{vellumory-thumbnail-unmark-all})
@kindex U
@findex vellumory-thumbnail-unmark-all
Clear every mark.
@item @kbd{x} (@code{vellumory-thumbnail-export-marked})
@kindex x
@findex vellumory-thumbnail-export-marked
Export the marked pages.
@end table

The export menu (@code{x} in the command menu) offers all of this too: @code{p} exports
this page@comma{} @code{x} extracts pages@comma{} @code{s} splits@comma{} @code{m} marks or unmarks this page@comma{} @code{e}
exports the marked pages@comma{} @code{U} clears the marks@comma{} @code{t} exports the text and @code{T}
shows the text of this page.  The right-click menu on a page offers
exporting it and marking it.

@node Comparing versions
@chapter Comparing versions

@cindex comparing versions
@cindex diff
@findex vellumory-diff-mode

When a document comes back from a colleague@comma{} or after a series of edits@comma{}
you want to know what changed.  Vellumory compares what the document says@comma{}
not its bytes.

@table @asis
@item @kbd{d} (@code{vellumory-diff})
@kindex d
@findex vellumory-diff
Compare an earlier version (a file you choose)
  with this document@comma{} and show the result in @samp{*Diff: NAME*}.
@end table

From the history tree@comma{} @code{d} compares any recorded state with the current file
(see @ref{The history tree}).

How the comparison works:

@itemize
@item
Pages are paired by their text@comma{} so an inserted or deleted page does not
make every later page look different.
@item
Paired pages are compared word by word.  Added words are highlighted in
the reader (the face @code{vellumory-highlight-added}) for as long as the
comparison buffer exists; removed words are listed.
@item
Paired pages are also compared by their rotation and their media@comma{} crop@comma{}
bleed@comma{} trim and art boxes@comma{} so a turned page shows up as@comma{} for example@comma{}
"rotation 0° → 90°"@comma{} even when its words are unchanged.
@item
Metadata@comma{} attachments and structure are compared field by field.
@end itemize

In the comparison buffer:

@table @asis
@item @kbd{n} (@code{vellumory-diff-next})
@kindex n
@findex vellumory-diff-next
Next changed page.
@item @kbd{p} (@code{vellumory-diff-previous})
@kindex p
@findex vellumory-diff-previous
Previous changed page.
@item @kbd{@key{TAB}}
@kindex TAB
Next button.
@end table

@code{vellumory diff OLD NEW} prints the same comparison as JSON@.

@node Org mode and @LaTeX{}
@chapter Org mode and @LaTeX{}

@cindex Org mode
@cindex LaTeX

Vellumory fits into the way many Emacs users write: notes in Org@comma{} papers
in @LaTeX{}.  Links from your notes open the exact place in a document@comma{} Org
exports to PDF that is accessible and archival at once@comma{} @LaTeX{} sources and
their PDF point to each other@comma{} and org-noter sessions run on Vellumory.

@menu
* Org links::
* Exporting Org to accessible PDF::
* Exporting with Typst::
* The Org source inside the PDF::
* Sync@TeX{} and AUC@TeX{}::
* org-noter::
@end menu

@node Org links
@section Org links

@cindex Org links
@cindex vellumory links
@cindex org-store-link

Org learns a new link type@comma{} @samp{vellumory:}@comma{} as soon as Org's link library
loads (after @code{vellumory-install}).

To link to a place@comma{} run @code{org-store-link} in a document@comma{} then insert the link
in your notes with @code{org-insert-link} (@code{C-c C-l}).  The link records the exact
place@comma{} page and height:

@example
[[vellumory:~/papers/paper.pdf::7::312.5][“the trace tree”]]
@end example

That is page 7@comma{} 312.5 points from the top of the page.  The description is
the selected text@comma{} if you selected some; otherwise the nearest heading of
the document's contents with the page label; otherwise the file name and
page.

Following the link opens the document at that place@comma{} and
@code{vellumory-history-back} returns to where you were.  When you insert a link
with @code{C-c C-l} and choose the @samp{vellumory:} type@comma{} Vellumory asks for the
document and the page.

When Org exports a @samp{vellumory:} link to HTML or Markdown@comma{} it becomes a link
to @samp{FILE#page=N}@comma{} which browsers open at the page; @LaTeX{} gets the same
through @samp{\href}.  Other back-ends get the description alone.

@cindex org-pdftools
Notes written with org-pdftools use @samp{pdf:} links@comma{} such as
@samp{pdf:paper.pdf::7++0.42}.  Vellumory opens them@comma{} at the page and the height
fraction@comma{} provided no other package has defined @samp{pdf:} links by the time
Vellumory's Org support loads (org-pdftools itself does@comma{} for pdf-tools).  Links to annotations or searches open their page.

@node Exporting Org to accessible PDF
@section Exporting Org to accessible PDF

@cindex Org export
@cindex accessible PDF
@cindex LuaLaTeX
@cindex tagged PDF

Vellumory adds an Org export that produces PDF 2.0 that is accessible and
archival at the same time: PDF/UA-2@comma{} with a tagged structure (and MathML
for formulas)@comma{} and PDF/A-4f.  It uses Lua@LaTeX{} with @LaTeX{}'s new
@samp{\DocumentMetadata} interface.

@table @asis
@item @kbd{C-c C-e l v}
@kindex C-c C-e l v
In the Org export dispatcher@comma{} the entry "As
  accessible@comma{} archival PDF (Vellumory@comma{} validated)".
@item @kbd{M-x vellumory-org-export-pdf}
@findex vellumory-org-export-pdf
The same as a command.  With a
  prefix argument@comma{} export only the subtree at point.
@end table

What happens:

@enumerate
@item
Org writes @LaTeX{} with a @samp{\DocumentMetadata} line naming the document's
language@comma{} the standards in @code{vellumory-org-export-standards} (by default
@samp{ua-2} and @samp{a-4f}) and tagging@comma{} and Lua@LaTeX{} compiles it.
@item
The Org source is attached to the PDF (see @ref{The Org source inside the PDF}).
@item
The PDF opens in Vellumory@comma{} and veraPDF validates it against each
standard.  The echo area reports the results@comma{} for example
@samp{UA-2 ✓@comma{} A-4F ✓}; a standard that is missed opens its validation report.
@end enumerate

The language comes from the file's @samp{#+LANGUAGE:} line@comma{} and must be a
language tag such as @samp{en-GB}; otherwise Vellumory asks you to fix it.  A
@samp{#+LATEX_CLASS_PRE:} line in the file takes precedence over Vellumory's
@samp{\DocumentMetadata} line@comma{} so you can set it yourself.  To target other
standards@comma{} change @code{vellumory-org-export-standards} (for example to
@code{("a-2b")}).

This needs Lua@LaTeX{} from @TeX{} Live 2025 or newer@comma{} with the @LaTeX{} tagging
code (the packages latex-lab@comma{} tagpdf@comma{} pdfmanagement-testphase and luamml).
The project's flake pins such a @TeX{} Live@comma{} and a test checks that veraPDF
accepts a real Org document (headings@comma{} a list@comma{} a table@comma{} a link@comma{} inline and
display mathematics) as PDF/UA-2@comma{} tagged@comma{} and PDF/A-4f.

@node Exporting with Typst
@section Exporting with Typst

@cindex Typst

If you prefer not to install @TeX{}@comma{} Typst can do the same job.

@table @asis
@item @kbd{C-c C-e y v}
@kindex C-c C-e y v
In the export dispatcher (with the ox-typst package)@comma{}
  the entry "As PDF/A-3a and PDF/UA-1 (Vellumory@comma{} validated)".
@item @kbd{M-x vellumory-org-export-typst}
@findex vellumory-org-export-typst
The same as a command.
@end table

ox-typst writes Typst@comma{} and @samp{typst compile --pdf-standard a-3a@comma{}ua-1} makes a
tagged PDF/A-3a that is also PDF/UA-1.  Typst refuses images without
alternative text@comma{} which is what accessibility asks for anyway.  The
language comes from @samp{#+LANGUAGE:}; a region such as @samp{en-GB} is passed the way
Typst wants it.  The result is validated against both standards and
opened@comma{} as with @LaTeX{}.  @code{vellumory-org-typst-standards} chooses the
standards; Typst's PDF/UA-1 cannot be combined with PDF/A-4.  This needs
Typst 0.14 or newer (0.15.1 is the tested version) and the ox-typst
package.

@node The Org source inside the PDF
@section The Org source inside the PDF

@cindex embedded source
@cindex associated files

Both export routes attach the Org text to the PDF as its source file: an
associated file with the relationship Source and the type @samp{text/x-org}.
Whoever has the PDF has what made it.  The text is the buffer as you
exported it@comma{} saved or not.

The attachment is an ordinary edit@comma{} so it appears in the PDF's history and
can be undone.  Standards that forbid it (PDF/A-1@comma{} PDF/A-2 and plain
PDF/A-4) get no attachment.  @code{vellumory-org-export-embed-source} turns this
off.

@node Sync@TeX{} and AUC@TeX{}
@section Sync@TeX{} and AUC@TeX{}

@cindex SyncTeX
@cindex AUCTeX
@cindex forward search
@cindex backward search

Sync@TeX{} connects a @LaTeX{} source and its PDF: from a line of source to the
place it appears@comma{} and back.  Vellumory reads Sync@TeX{} data with @TeX{} Live's
libsynctex@comma{} the parser that Skim@comma{} Sumatra and Texworks use.  Compile with
Sync@TeX{} on (in AUC@TeX{}@comma{} turn on @code{TeX-source-correlate-mode})@comma{} so that a
@samp{.synctex.gz} file lies next to the PDF@.

With AUC@TeX{}@comma{} Vellumory becomes the PDF viewer when a @TeX{} buffer starts
(@code{vellumory-sync-tex-viewer}).  It sets itself up after every other viewer
configuration@comma{} Doom's @samp{:lang latex} included.

@table @asis
@item @kbd{C-c C-v}
@kindex C-c C-v
In a @TeX{} buffer@comma{} AUC@TeX{}'s @code{TeX-view}: show the PDF with the
  line at point highlighted on its page.  This needs
  @code{TeX-source-correlate-mode}; without it@comma{} the PDF simply opens.  Region
  compilation works too.
@item @kbd{M-x vellumory-sync-forward}
@findex vellumory-sync-forward
The same without AUC@TeX{}: show where the
  source line at point appears.  The PDF is the file of the same name with
  the extension @samp{.pdf}.
@item @kbd{C-mouse-1} (@code{vellumory-sync-backward})
@kindex C-mouse-1
@findex vellumory-sync-backward
In a document@comma{} visit the
  source line that produced the point you click.
@end table

The found lines are highlighted for @code{vellumory-sync-flash-seconds}@comma{} and
@code{vellumory-sync-context-points} of the page are kept visible above them.
If the source file has moved@comma{} Vellumory looks for a file of the same name
next to the PDF@.

@node org-noter
@section org-noter

@cindex org-noter

org-noter keeps notes in step with a document.  Once org-noter is loaded@comma{}
its sessions run on Vellumory documents:

@itemize
@item
the notes follow the page in view;
@item
a note opens its page@comma{} at its height;
@item
precise notes start at the selected text@comma{} or at a click on any visible
page@comma{} not only the top one;
@item
the selected text starts the note's text;
@item
@code{org-noter-create-skeleton} turns the document's contents into headings.
@end itemize

Locations are stored in org-noter's own form@comma{} so notes made with other
org-noter back-ends keep working.  With evil@comma{} org-noter's @code{i}@comma{} @code{C-i} and @code{q}
take precedence over the reader's keys during a session.

@node Batch work in Dired
@chapter Batch work in Dired

@cindex Dired
@cindex batch processing
@findex vellumory-batch-mode

Mark documents in Dired@comma{} then press @code{;} followed by a letter
(@code{vellumory-dired-key} changes the prefix).

@table @asis
@item @kbd{; v} (@code{vellumory-dired-validate})
@kindex ; v
@findex vellumory-dired-validate
Validate the marked documents
  against one profile@comma{} in a single veraPDF run.
@item @kbd{; e} (@code{vellumory-dired-edit})
@kindex ; e
@findex vellumory-dired-edit
Apply one edit to each marked
  document: compress losslessly@comma{} linearize@comma{} recognize text@comma{} set the
  language@comma{} set the author@comma{} show the title in viewers' title bars@comma{} or
  remove page labels.  Each edit is recorded in that document's own history
  and can be undone there.
@item @kbd{; i} (@code{vellumory-dired-info})
@kindex ; i
@findex vellumory-dired-info
Summarize the marked documents: pages@comma{}
  format@comma{} claimed standards@comma{} tagging@comma{} language and size.
@item @kbd{; m} (@code{vellumory-dired-merge})
@kindex ; m
@findex vellumory-dired-merge
Merge the marked documents@comma{} in Dired's
  order@comma{} into a new document.  Document-level data@comma{} such as metadata@comma{} comes
  from the first.
@item @kbd{; o} (@code{vellumory-occur-documents})
@kindex ; o
@findex vellumory-occur-documents
List the lines matching a regular
  expression in all marked documents (see @ref{Listing every match}).
@item @kbd{; V} (@code{vellumory-virtual-create})
@kindex ; V
@findex vellumory-virtual-create
Start a virtual document that reads
  the marked documents in order (see @ref{Virtual documents}).
@end table

The results appear in a table@comma{} @samp{*Vellumory batch*}:

@table @asis
@item @kbd{@key{RET}} (@code{vellumory-batch-visit})
@kindex RET
@findex vellumory-batch-visit
Open the document on this line.
@item @kbd{v} (@code{vellumory-batch-report})
@kindex v
@findex vellumory-batch-report
Show the full validation report of the
  document on this line.
@item @kbd{g} (@code{vellumory-batch-rerun})
@kindex g
@findex vellumory-batch-rerun
Run the batch again.
@end table

In a validation batch@comma{} a file that cannot be read still gets its row@comma{}
with the error.  In Doom@comma{}
the same commands are on the localleader under @code{v} (see @ref{The Doom module}).

@node The command line
@chapter The command line

@cindex command line
@cindex scripting

The worker is also a command line tool.  Everything the reader does with
documents@comma{} scripts can do too@comma{} with the same results and the same
history.

@example
vellumory [SUBCOMMAND] [FLAGS]
@end example

@samp{vellumory --help} lists the subcommands@comma{} and @samp{vellumory SUBCOMMAND --help}
describes each one.  @samp{--version} prints the worker's version.

Most subcommands print JSON on standard output.  When something goes
wrong@comma{} they print @samp{@{"error": "…"@}} on standard error and exit with status 1.
The exit status tells you whether the command worked@comma{} not what it found:
a document that fails validation still exits with 0@comma{} and the @samp{compliant}
field of the report gives the verdict.

