* Inspecting from the command line::
* Editing from the command line::
* Edit operations::
* Pages in and out::
* Sync@TeX{} from the command line::
* For developers::
* Environment variables::
@end menu

@node Inspecting from the command line
@section Inspecting from the command line

@cindex command line, inspecting

@table @asis
@item @code{vellumory info FILE}
The details report: format@comma{} structure@comma{} metadata@comma{}
fonts@comma{} images and attachments@comma{} as JSON@.
@item @code{vellumory text FILE}
The text of every page; pages are separated by
form feeds.
@item @code{vellumory annotations FILE}
The annotations (not links or form fields)
with their pages@comma{} as JSON@.
@item @code{vellumory form FILE}
The form fields: name@comma{} type@comma{} value and choices.
@item @code{vellumory signatures FILE}
Verify every signature: integrity of the
signed bytes@comma{} trust of the signer.
@item @code{vellumory preflight FILE}
The checks beyond veraPDF (see @ref{Checks beyond veraPDF}).
@item @code{vellumory validate [-f FLAVOUR] FILE}
Validate with veraPDF and print a
normalized report.  The flavours are @samp{0} (detect from the document's
metadata@comma{} the default)@comma{} @samp{1a} @samp{1b} @samp{2a} @samp{2b} @samp{2u} @samp{3a} @samp{3b} @samp{3u} @samp{4} @samp{4e} @samp{4f}
@samp{ua1} @samp{ua2} @samp{wt1r} @samp{wt1a}.
@item @code{vellumory batch-validate [-f FLAVOUR] FILE…}
Validate many documents in
one veraPDF run and print one result per file.  With @samp{0}@comma{} each file is
checked against its own claim.
@item @code{vellumory check FILE}
qpdf's structural check@comma{} with qpdf's own output
and exit codes (0 for clean@comma{} 2 for errors@comma{} 3 for warnings).
@item @code{vellumory qdf FILE}
Write the editable QDF rendering to the cache and
print its path.
@item @code{vellumory diff OLD NEW}
The semantic comparison (see @ref{Comparing versions}): pages paired@comma{} changed words with their positions@comma{} structure.
@end table

For example@comma{} the beginning of a validation report:

@example
$ vellumory validate -f 1b paper.pdf
@{"compliant":false@comma{}"profile":"PDF/A-1b validation profile"@comma{}
 "statement":"PDF file is not compliant with Validation Profile requirements."@comma{}
 "passed_rules":123@comma{}"failed_rules":6@comma{} …@}
@end example

@node Editing from the command line
@section Editing from the command line

@cindex command line, editing

@table @asis
@item @code{vellumory edit FILE OPERATION}
Apply a recorded@comma{} reversible edit@comma{} given
as JSON (next section).  The file is replaced atomically@comma{} and every state
stays in the store.  Write @samp{-} instead of the operation to read it from
standard input; operations that carry passwords should always be passed
that way.
@item @code{vellumory undo FILE}
Restore the state before the last edit.
@item @code{vellumory redo FILE}
Return to the most recent state after the current
one.
@item @code{vellumory history FILE}
Print the branching history as JSON: every
state with its index@comma{} parent@comma{} time@comma{} label@comma{} operation and hash.
@item @code{vellumory checkout FILE N}
Make state N current.  Nothing is discarded.
@item @code{vellumory snapshot FILE N}
Print the path of state N's snapshot in the
store@comma{} without changing the file.
@end table

Each edit prints a short summary@comma{} such as:

@example
$ vellumory edit paper.pdf '@{"op":"rotate"@comma{}"pages":[1]@comma{}"degrees":90@}'
@{"changed":true@comma{}"head":1@comma{}"node":@{"hash":"a428dc90…"@comma{}"parent":0@comma{}
 "operation":@{"op":"rotate"@comma{}"pages":[1]@comma{}"degrees":90@}@comma{}
 "label":"rotate pages 1 by +90° (14 pages)"@comma{}"index":1@comma{}…@}@comma{}"nodes":2@}
@end example

The reader and the command line share the store and the history@comma{} so an
edit made in a script can be undone in Emacs@comma{} and the other way round.

@node Edit operations
@section Edit operations

@cindex edit operations
@cindex JSON operations

An operation is a JSON object whose @samp{op} field names it.  Pages are
numbered from 1.

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem @samp{op}
@tab Fields
@item @samp{select}
@tab @samp{pages}: the pages to keep@comma{} in order (reorders@comma{} deletes@comma{} duplicates)
@item @samp{rotate}
@tab @samp{pages}@comma{} @samp{degrees} (a multiple of 90; positive is clockwise)
@item @samp{append}
@tab @samp{files}: documents whose pages are appended
@item @samp{compress}
@tab none
@item @samp{linearize}
@tab none
@item @samp{labels}
@tab @samp{specs}: qpdf page-label specifications@comma{} such as @samp{["1:r"@comma{} "5:D"]}
@item @samp{remove-labels}
@tab none
@item @samp{attach}
@tab @samp{file}@comma{} @samp{key}@comma{} optional @samp{description} and @samp{relationship}
@item @samp{detach}
@tab @samp{key}
@item @samp{metadata}
@tab @samp{set} (an object)@comma{} @samp{remove} (a list)@comma{} @samp{date} (ISO 8601)
@item @samp{ocr}
@tab @samp{languages} (default @samp{eng})@comma{} @samp{mode} (@samp{skip}@comma{} @samp{redo} or @samp{force})
@item @samp{stamp}
@tab @samp{file}@comma{} @samp{under} (default false)@comma{} @samp{pages} (default @samp{1-z})
@item @samp{encrypt}
@tab @samp{user}@comma{} @samp{owner}@comma{} @samp{print} (@samp{full}@comma{} @samp{low}@comma{} @samp{none})@comma{} @samp{modify}@comma{} @samp{extract}
@item @samp{decrypt}
@tab @samp{password}
@item @samp{fill-form}
@tab @samp{values}: an object of field names and values
@item @samp{annotate}
@tab @samp{page}@comma{} @samp{kind}@comma{} @samp{quads} or @samp{rect}@comma{} @samp{color}@comma{} @samp{contents}@comma{} @samp{author}@comma{} @samp{time}
@item @samp{annotation-edit}
@tab @samp{page}@comma{} @samp{object}@comma{} @samp{contents}@comma{} @samp{time}
@item @samp{annotation-delete}
@tab @samp{page}@comma{} @samp{object}
@item @samp{syntax}
@tab @samp{qdf}: the path of an edited QDF file
@end multitable

The metadata fields are @samp{title}@comma{} @samp{author}@comma{} @samp{subject}@comma{} @samp{keywords}@comma{} @samp{creator}@comma{}
@samp{producer}@comma{} @samp{language} (a language tag such as @samp{en-GB})@comma{} @samp{display-title}
(@samp{"true"} or @samp{"false"}) and @samp{created} (an ISO 8601 date).  @samp{modify} is one of
@samp{all}@comma{} @samp{annotate}@comma{} @samp{form}@comma{} @samp{assembly} or @samp{none}@comma{} and @samp{extract} (true or false)
allows copying.  Annotation kinds are @samp{Highlight}@comma{} @samp{Underline}@comma{} @samp{StrikeOut}@comma{}
@samp{Squiggly} (on @samp{quads}@comma{} eight numbers per marked line) and @samp{Text} (at @samp{rect}).

Some examples:

@example
vellumory edit report.pdf '@{"op":"select"@comma{}"pages":[3@comma{}1@comma{}2]@}'
vellumory edit report.pdf '@{"op":"metadata"@comma{}"set":@{"title":"Annual report"@comma{}"language":"en-GB"@}@comma{}"remove":["keywords"]@comma{}"date":"2026-10-28T06:00:00+01:00"@}'
vellumory edit scan.pdf '@{"op":"ocr"@comma{}"languages":"deu+eng"@comma{}"mode":"skip"@}'
echo '@{"op":"encrypt"@comma{}"user":""@comma{}"owner":"secret"@comma{}"print":"full"@comma{}"modify":"none"@comma{}"extract":true@}' | vellumory edit report.pdf -
@end example

qpdf receives passwords through private files@comma{} never on its command line.

@node Pages in and out
@section Pages in and out

@cindex merging documents

@table @asis
@item @code{vellumory extract FILE PAGES OUTPUT}
Write the pages to a new document@comma{}
keeping outlines and tags.  @samp{PAGES} is a qpdf page range@comma{} such as @samp{1-3@comma{}7}@comma{}
@samp{r1} (the last page) or @samp{1-z:odd}.
@item @code{vellumory split FILE N PATTERN}
Split into documents of N pages; @samp{%d} in
the pattern becomes the page range@comma{} as in @samp{out/part-%d.pdf}.
@item @code{vellumory merge OUTPUT FILE…}
Concatenate documents into a new one;
document-level data comes from the first.
@item @code{vellumory compose}
Compose pages of several documents into one@comma{} as
virtual documents do.  It reads a JSON list of parts from standard input@comma{}
each with @samp{file}@comma{} @samp{first}@comma{} @samp{last} (0 for the last page) and an optional
@samp{region} of four fractions (left@comma{} top@comma{} right@comma{} bottom)@comma{} and prints the path
of the cached result.
@end table

These leave their inputs unchanged.

@node Sync@TeX{} from the command line
@section Sync@TeX{} from the command line

@table @asis
@item @code{vellumory synctex [-c COLUMN] FILE SOURCE LINE}
Where a source line
appears in the PDF (forward search).  @samp{FILE} is the PDF@comma{} next to its
@samp{.synctex.gz}.
@item @code{vellumory synctexback FILE PAGE X Y}
Which source produced a point on a
page (backward search)@comma{} with @samp{X} and @samp{Y} in points from the left and top
edges.
@end table

@node For developers
@section For developers

@table @asis
@item @code{vellumory serve}
Serve the reader's protocol@comma{} JSON lines on standard
input and output.  This is how Emacs talks to the worker; the protocol is
internal and may change between versions.
@item @code{vellumory clip HEIGHT TOP BOTTOM}
Plan a clipped raster interval with
the proved integer geometry; a small demonstration of the proofs at
work.
@end table

@node Environment variables
@section Environment variables

@cindex environment variables
@cindex XDG directories

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Variable
@tab Meaning
@item @samp{VELLUMORY_STORE}
@tab where snapshots and histories live (default @samp{$XDG_DATA_HOME/vellumory})
@item @samp{XDG_DATA_HOME}
@tab base of the store (default @samp{~/.local/share})
@item @samp{XDG_CACHE_HOME}
@tab base of the worker's caches: previews@comma{} QDF@comma{} virtual documents@comma{} attachments
@item @samp{XDG_RUNTIME_DIR}
@tab where the session's rendered strips are kept (falls back to the cache)
@item @samp{VELLUMORY_RENDER_THREADS}
@tab render threads@comma{} 1 to 16 (default: cores minus two@comma{} from 2 to 6)
@item @samp{VELLUMORY_RUNTIME_BUDGET_MB}
@tab most memory for the session's strips (default 128@comma{} and at most a quarter of the free space there)
@item @samp{VELLUMORY_PERSISTENT_BUDGET_MB}
@tab most disk for previews kept across sessions (default 256)
@end multitable

The reader's own files (saved places@comma{} and the cache of remote documents
and conversions) follow @code{vellumory-state-directory} and
@code{vellumory-cache-directory} instead (see @ref{Customization}).

@node Evil and Doom Emacs
@chapter Evil and Doom Emacs

@cindex evil
@cindex vim keys

Vellumory has full evil support@comma{} modelled on evil-collection's keys for
pdf-tools@comma{} and a Doom Emacs module that wires everything together.

