@node What Vellumory changes in Emacs
@section What Vellumory changes in Emacs

@cindex advice
@cindex remapped commands

So that nothing surprises you@comma{} here is every place where Vellumory
changes how Emacs itself behaves:

@itemize
@item
@code{vellumory-install} adds a file name handler that visits documents
without reading their bytes into the buffer (only for whole-file visits
of files that open in Vellumory; @code{vellumory-visit-without-reading})@comma{} and
advises @code{abort-if-file-too-large} to skip the large-file question for
documents.
@item
@code{recenter} is advised: in a window showing a document it keeps the place
instead of scrolling.  Doom's window splits and evil's @code{zz} call it.
@item
Reader buffers turn off @code{bidi-display-reordering}@comma{} which they do not need@comma{}
and @code{auto-hscroll-mode}@comma{} because Vellumory scrolls sideways itself.
@item
While the view moves@comma{} @code{gc-cons-threshold} is raised to
@code{vellumory-gc-threshold}@comma{} and garbage is collected once reading rests (see
@code{vellumory-gc-idle-delay}).  With @code{gcmh-mode} on@comma{} as in Doom@comma{} collection is
left to it.
@item
@code{save-place-mode} is off for documents@comma{} and their old save-place entries
are dropped.
@item
In documents@comma{} these commands are remapped: @code{write-file} to
@code{vellumory-write-copy}@comma{} @code{undo} and @code{undo-only} to @code{vellumory-undo}@comma{} @code{undo-redo}
to @code{vellumory-redo}@comma{} @code{text-scale-adjust} to @code{vellumory-text-scale-adjust}@comma{}
@code{keyboard-quit} to @code{vellumory-search-quit}@comma{} and @code{consult-line} and Doom's
@code{+default/search-buffer} to @code{vellumory-consult-line}.
@item
Turning on @code{follow-mode} in a document turns on @code{vellumory-follow-mode}
instead.
@item
A function on @code{TeX-mode-hook} makes Vellumory AUC@TeX{}'s PDF viewer after
every other setup; @code{vellumory-sync-tex-viewer} set to @code{nil} keeps yours.
@item
A function on @code{enable-theme-functions} redraws documents whose page
colours follow the Emacs theme.
@item
With evil@comma{} evil-snipe is turned off in documents and their sidebars.
@end itemize

@node Performance and how it is measured
@chapter Performance and how it is measured

@cindex performance
@cindex benchmarks
@cindex speed

Speed claims are only worth something with the method behind them.  This
chapter gives both@comma{} and says where the evidence ends.

@menu
* What makes it fast::
* The method::
* Results::
* The limits of this evidence::
@end menu

@node What makes it fast
@section What makes it fast

@itemize
@item
Rendering happens in the worker@comma{} on several threads@comma{} while Emacs stays
free for your input.  Only strips that a window shows are rendered sharp@comma{}
at the screen's resolution.  Work that scrolls out of view is cancelled.
@item
The worker writes raw pixels@comma{} which Emacs decodes fastest@comma{} to a directory
in memory; nothing is compressed to PNG on the way.
@item
While the view moves faster than rendering@comma{} previews fill in; cheap
pages render sharp at once; idle time prepares what comes next.
@item
Images rendered ahead are decoded before they scroll into view@comma{} not in
the step that shows them.
@item
Reader buffers turn off Emacs's bidirectional text reordering@comma{} which
they do not need: that cut the redisplay time of a scroll step from
1.27 ms to 0.52 ms.
@item
Garbage collection is held back while you scroll and runs once reading
rests@comma{} so it does not fall into a step.
@item
Opening reads page sizes from the document's page tree without parsing
page contents@comma{} and hashes the file on another thread while MuPDF parses
it.
@end itemize

@node The method
@section The method

@cindex measurement method

Two benchmarks are part of the repository and run from the development
shell.

The first@comma{} @samp{tests/bench.el}@comma{} runs in a real graphical Emacs and drives each
reader through the same script on the same document and window.
"Blocking" is the time Emacs's main thread spends in a command plus a
forced redisplay: the delay a keypress experiences.  "Sharp" is the time
until every visible part of the window shows a final rendering.  Each run
uses a freshly started Emacs with byte-compiled code.

@example
lean --run scripts/Build.lean lake env lean --run scripts/Review.lean \
  bench target/corpus/pdf32000-2008.pdf
@end example

The second@comma{} the screen benchmark (@samp{Review.lean screenbench}@comma{} with
@samp{tests/screen-bench.el})@comma{} measures what reaches the display.  It records the
page area at 200 frames a second with ffmpeg while each viewer follows the
same script@comma{} and measures from the start of each action to the first
changed frame@comma{} and to the last change before the next action.  This
includes viewers that draw outside Emacs@comma{} such as EAF@.

All figures below come from one machine: an AMD Ryzen 5 3600 (12
threads)@comma{} with Emacs on a virtual X display (Xvfb).  They are ranges over
consecutive runs.

