@node The limits of this evidence
@section The limits of this evidence

@cindex limitations of the measurements

@itemize
@item
Every figure comes from one server on a virtual display.  No laptop or
physical screen has been measured@comma{} and HiDPI has been checked only at
GTK's scale 2 on a virtual display.
@item
The pgtk build of Emacs running on X11 added about 8 ms to every Emacs
viewer's step: on the pinned Emacs 32 pgtk@comma{} forced onto X11@comma{} Vellumory's
median step was 11.8–13.7 ms and its worst 19.9 ms@comma{} and the order of the
viewers held.  Under Wayland@comma{} pgtk's own display@comma{} no screen recording was
made.
@item
The numbers are for these documents and these scripts.  Other documents@comma{}
with heavier pages@comma{} behave differently.
@item
Interpreted Lisp roughly doubles the layout and redisplay work@comma{} so the
measurements are only meaningful with byte-compiled code.
@end itemize

The full record@comma{} with every run and the reasoning behind each design
choice@comma{} is in the repository: @samp{docs/reader-validation.org} and
@samp{docs/research.org}.

@node Troubleshooting
@chapter Troubleshooting

@cindex troubleshooting
@cindex problems

Most problems have a simple cause.  Start with @code{M-x vellumory-doctor}: it
checks the whole setup@comma{} names what is wrong and@comma{} where Emacs can fix it@comma{}
offers a button (see @ref{Checking that it works}).  Here are the problems
you are most likely to meet.

@menu
* The document opens in another mode::
* Emacs is too old::
* Emacs says it needs a graphical display::
* The header line says the worker stopped::
* An edit says the file is read-only::
* Other edit refusals::
* Search finds nothing::
* Validation@comma{} OCR or export complain about a missing program: Validation OCR or export complain about a missing program. 
* Pages look blurry for a moment::
* Sync@TeX{} finds nothing::
* vellumory-info cannot find the manual::
* Evil keys do not work::
* Where Vellumory keeps its files::
@end menu

@node The document opens in another mode
@section The document opens in another mode

@cindex wrong mode

@itemize
@item
@code{vellumory-install} has not run.  Call it in your init file (see @ref{Setting up your init file})@comma{} or open the file with @code{M-x vellumory-open}.
@item
Another reader was installed after it.  pdf-tools' @code{pdf-tools-install}@comma{} for
example@comma{} puts its own association in front.  Call @code{vellumory-install}
afterwards.
@item
Your package manager does not load the generated autoloads.  Add
@code{(require 'vellumory-autoloads)} before @code{(vellumory-install)}.  In a plain
clone@comma{} generate the file first (see @ref{Setting up your init file}).
@end itemize

@node Emacs is too old
@section Emacs is too old

Vellumory needs Emacs 29.1 or newer@comma{} and the doctor says so.  Exporting
Org to accessible PDF also needs Org 9.7 or newer; the export names the
version it found (see @ref{Requirements}).

@node Emacs says it needs a graphical display
@section Emacs says it needs a graphical display

The message reads "Vellumory needs a graphical Emacs with image support".
Pages are images@comma{} so start Emacs on a graphical display; @code{emacs -nw} and
text terminals cannot show them.

@node The header line says the worker stopped
@section The header line says the worker stopped

@cindex worker stopped
@cindex diagnostics

The header line reads "Worker stopped:" followed by the reason: the worker
program has ended.  Its messages are in the hidden buffer
@samp{" *Vellumory worker diagnostics*"} (the name starts with a space; type it
with @code{C-x b} and a leading space).  Press @code{r} to reload: that starts a new
worker.  If the worker cannot start at all@comma{} check @code{vellumory-program}: in a
shell@comma{} @samp{vellumory --help} should print the subcommands.

@node An edit says the file is read-only
@section An edit says the file is read-only

Edits replace the file@comma{} so they need write access to it and to its
directory.  @code{C-x C-w} (@code{vellumory-write-copy}) writes a writable copy and
opens it; edit that (see @ref{Documents that cannot be edited}).

@node Other edit refusals
@section Other edit refusals

@itemize
@item
"Only documents visited from a local file can be edited": a remote file
or a buffer without a file.  @code{C-x C-w} writes a local copy.
@item
"…is shown converted to PDF": converted documents cannot be edited.
@item
"A virtual document is composed from others": edit the sources.
@item
"A document needs at least one page": you tried to delete every page.
@item
"PDF/A-1 forbids the transparency highlights need": underline instead
(@code{a u}).
@item
A question about a digital signature: see @ref{Signatures}.
@end itemize

@node Search finds nothing
@section Search finds nothing

The document may have no text layer: scans are pictures of text.  Add one
with @code{e o} (which needs ocrmypdf and Tesseract with your language's data).
If the text is there but search still misses it@comma{} try a regular expression
search (@code{C-M-s}); some documents encode characters oddly@comma{} and the preflight
checks (@code{V}@comma{} then @code{b}) report fonts without Unicode maps.

@node Validation OCR or export complain about a missing program
@section Validation@comma{} OCR or export complain about a missing program

Each of these runs a separate program: veraPDF for validation@comma{} ocrmypdf
for text recognition@comma{} Lua@LaTeX{} or Typst for Org export@comma{} a converter for
DVI and the others.  Install the one named in the message (see
@ref{Requirements}).  The Nix build of the worker includes qpdf@comma{} veraPDF and
ocrmypdf; other builds look for them on your @samp{PATH}.  In Doom@comma{} @samp{doom doctor}
lists what is missing.

For the @LaTeX{} export@comma{} @TeX{} Live must be 2025 or newer and include the
tagging code; the message "#+LANGUAGE must be a language tag" means that
the file's @samp{#+LANGUAGE:} line should read like @samp{en-GB}.

@node Pages look blurry for a moment
@section Pages look blurry for a moment

That is the preview shown while you move faster than pages can be
rendered.  It sharpens about a tenth of a second after you stop
(@code{vellumory-refine-delay}).  If pages stay blurry@comma{} the worker may be busy
with a heavy page; the header line says "Rendering…" until it is done.

@node Sync@TeX{} finds nothing
@section Sync@TeX{} finds nothing

Compile with Sync@TeX{} on@comma{} so that @samp{NAME.synctex.gz} lies next to @samp{NAME.pdf}.
In AUC@TeX{}@comma{} turn on @code{TeX-source-correlate-mode} before compiling.  Forward
search names the source line it looked for when it finds no match.

@node vellumory-info cannot find the manual
@section vellumory-info cannot find the manual

Info has to find @samp{vellumory.info}.  See @ref{Reading this manual in Emacs}.

@node Evil keys do not work
@section Evil keys do not work

Call @code{vellumory-evil-setup} after evil has loaded (see @ref{Setting up evil}).
In Doom@comma{} enable @samp{:editor evil} and the module does it.

@node Where Vellumory keeps its files
@section Where Vellumory keeps its files

@cindex disk usage
@cindex files Vellumory writes

@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem What
@tab Where (by default)
@item reading positions
@tab @samp{~/.local/state/vellumory}
@item snapshots and edit histories
@tab @samp{~/.local/share/vellumory}
@item previews kept across sessions (256 MB at most)
@tab @samp{~/.cache/vellumory/rasters}
@item QDF files@comma{} virtual documents@comma{} attachments
@tab @samp{~/.cache/vellumory}
@item copies of remote documents@comma{} conversions
@tab @samp{~/.cache/vellumory}
@item the session's rendered strips
@tab under @samp{$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/vellumory}@comma{} removed on exit
@end multitable

Caches can be deleted at any time.  The store in @samp{~/.local/share/vellumory}
is not a cache: it holds your edit histories.

@node Frequently asked questions
@chapter Frequently asked questions

@cindex questions
@cindex FAQ

@menu
* Does reading a document change the file?::
* Can I use Vellumory alongside pdf-tools?::
* How much disk space does the history take?::
* Can I edit the text on a page?::
* Does it work on macOS or Windows?::
* Can I print?::
* Can I sign a document?::
* Why is the worker written in Lean?::
* Why does my EPUB have different page numbers after changing the text size?::
@end menu

@node Does reading a document change the file?
@section Does reading a document change the file?

No.  Reading never writes to the document.  Your place is saved elsewhere
(see @ref{Leaving and coming back})@comma{} and saving the buffer writes nothing.
The file changes only when you make an edit@comma{} and every edit can be
undone.

@node Can I use Vellumory alongside pdf-tools?
@section Can I use Vellumory alongside pdf-tools?

Yes.  Whichever was installed last opens PDFs by default@comma{} and @code{M-x
vellumory-open} always opens a file in Vellumory.  Vellumory reads pdf-tools'
@samp{.vpdf} virtual documents@comma{} and opens org-pdftools' @samp{pdf:} links when nothing
else claims them.

@node How much disk space does the history take?
@section How much disk space does the history take?

Every state is a complete copy of the document@comma{} stored once however often
it recurs.  A 2 MB paper edited ten times takes up to 22 MB in the store.
There is no command to prune the store yet.

@node Can I edit the text on a page?
@section Can I edit the text on a page?

No.  Vellumory edits a document's structure@comma{} not its page content.  For
the raw PDF@comma{} the syntax view (@code{C-c C-c}) lets an expert change objects
directly.

@node Does it work on macOS or Windows?
@section Does it work on macOS or Windows?

Nobody has tried yet.  Vellumory is developed and tested on Linux.  The Nix
flake also declares Apple silicon Macs (@samp{aarch64-darwin})@comma{} and CI builds it on
macOS 26 and runs its tests there on every change: the Lean suites@comma{} the proof
audit@comma{} the Emacs tests@comma{} and the graphical tests in Emacs's Cocoa build (all
pass but one@comma{} which needs a wider screen than the runner has).  No person has
used it on a Mac yet.
Intel Macs are out@comma{} since nixpkgs 26.11 dropped
them.  Reports from other systems are welcome.

@node Can I print?
@section Can I print?

Not from Vellumory: there is no print command yet.  Print the file with
your system's tools.

@node Can I sign a document?
@section Can I sign a document?

No.  Vellumory verifies signatures@comma{} but cannot create them.

@node Why is the worker written in Lean?
@section Why is the worker written in Lean?

Lean 4 is a programming language and a proof assistant.  The worker is
ordinary@comma{} fast compiled code@comma{} and some of its parts come with proofs: that
the render queue hands out the most urgent job and loses none when work is
cancelled@comma{} that the edit history stays well-formed@comma{} that clipped pixels
stay inside the page and the window@comma{} that arguments reach external
programs unchanged@comma{} and that the comparison's alignment is sound.  The
project's tooling (build@comma{} tests@comma{} reviews@comma{} benchmarks) is Lean too.

@node Why does my EPUB have different page numbers after changing the text size?
@section Why does my EPUB have different page numbers after changing the text size?

A book has no fixed pages; Vellumory lays it out on pages of a given size.
Larger text needs more pages.  Vellumory keeps you at the same share of
the book.

@node Credits
@chapter Credits

@cindex credits
@cindex acknowledgements

Vellumory stands on the work of many people.

It renders and reads documents with MuPDF@comma{} by Artifex.  Its edits run on
qpdf@comma{} by Jay Berkenbilt@comma{} whose fix-qdf also makes the syntax view
editable.  Validation is done by veraPDF@.  Text recognition is ocrmypdf with Tesseract.  Signatures
are verified with OpenSSL@comma{} XMP metadata is edited with Exempi and Adobe's
XMP Toolkit@comma{} and Sync@TeX{} data is read with @TeX{} Live's libsynctex.  The
worker is written in Lean 4@comma{} with Lake and the lean4-cli library.  Org
export uses Lua@LaTeX{} with the @LaTeX{} Project's tagging code@comma{} and Typst.
And all of it lives in GNU Emacs@comma{} with Org and transient.

The test documents include @samp{tracemonkey.pdf} and @samp{alphatrans.pdf} from
Mozilla's pdf.js test suite@comma{} and Adobe's published copy of ISO 32000-1.

Vellumory learned from the Emacs document readers before it@comma{} and is
grateful to their authors: DocView@comma{} which comes with Emacs; pdf-tools@comma{}
whose features and file formats set the bar@comma{} and whose users' wishes
shaped this work; emacs-reader@comma{} which showed what a MuPDF module can do;
EAF's PDF viewer; doc-tools; and org-noter@comma{} whose notes Vellumory is glad
to serve.  Its evil keys follow evil-collection's for pdf-tools@comma{} and its
presentation and caret browsing follow Evince@comma{} Okular and Firefox@comma{} and
its link previews GNOME's Papers.  Reading mode lays out its text with
Emacs's own HTML renderer@comma{} shr.  Fitting
the content width follows pdf-tools' slicing and Sioyek's smart fit.  The
rendering design learned from PDF.js@comma{} Chromium's PDFium viewer@comma{} Okular@comma{}
Evince@comma{} zathura and Sioyek.  The Doom module builds on Doom Emacs and
nix-doom-emacs-unstraightened.

